N2  - Objectives: Clopidogrel, prasugrel, and ticagrelor are widely prescribed oral P2Y12 receptor inhibitors of antiplatelet therapy. The metabolism of clopidogrel is influenced by CYP2C19 genotype. Platelet reactivity is a phenotypic indicator for ischemic events in patients receiving P2Y12 inhibitors. This study aims to develop and validate a model using patients' genotype and phenotype for projection of clinical outcomes of antiplatelet therapies in patients with acute coronary syndrome (ACS). Methods: A mathematical model was designed to simulate 12-month clinical event rates of two antiplatelet strategies including universal clopidogrel and universal alternative P2Y12 inhibitor (prasugrel or ticagrelor). In the model, patients were firstly differentiated by carriers/non-carriers of CYP2C19 loss-of-function allele and further by normal/low antiplatelet response. Model inputs were derived from published literature. The model validation was performed by comparing model results with published clinical data pooled from TRITON-TIMI 38 (Therapeutic Outcomes by Optimizing Platelet Inhibition with Prasugrel-Thrombolysis in Myocardial Infarction) and PLATO (Platelet Inhibition and Patient Outcomes) trials. Results: The simulated event rates of nonfatal myocardial infarction (7.39%), nonfatal stroke (0.87%), stent thrombosis (2.39%), cardiovascular death (3.71%), and major bleeding (4.52%) in universal clopidogrel arm were similar to the pooled data of clopidogrel in clinical trials (6.77%, 0.86%, 2.26%, 3.49%, and 4.57%, respectively). Similarly, the simulated event rates of nonfatal myocardial infarction (5.65%), nonfatal stroke (0.93%), stent thrombosis (1.37%), cardiovascular death (3.05%), and major bleeding (4.38%) in universal alternative P2Y12 inhibitor arm were consistent with data pooled from prasugrel and ticagrelor groups in the two clinical trials (5.48%, 0.90%, 1.43%, 2.85%, and 4.84%, respectively). The differences of clinical outcomes between model-based and clinical trials were within ±10%. Conclusions: The clinical outcomes of antiplatelet therapy simulated by a genotype-phenotype guided model were consistent to the pooled results from clinical trials in ACS patients.
