N2  - BACKGROUND: A significant number of patients with acute coronary syndromes (ACS) treated upfront with dual antiplatelet therapy (DAPT) subsequently require emergent or urgent coronary artery bypass grafting surgery (CABG). Clinical outcomes following CABG in relation to the timing of discontinuation of P2Y12 receptor antagonists have not been well studied. METHODS: We retrospectively identified patients with ACS undergoing urgent or emergent CABG after treatment with clopidogrel or ticagrelor. We then examined clinical outcomes after stratifying patients into three pre-specified time intervals from P2Y12 receptor antagonist discontinuation to CABG: <48 hours, 48 hours to <5 days, and ±5 days. The primary efficacy outcome for this analysis was the composite of death, reinfarction, need for revascularization, or stroke; the primary safety outcome was bleeding of at least moderate severity as defined by a Universal Definition of Perioperative Bleeding (UDPB) class ± 2. We performed two multivariable logistic regression analyses to examine the relationship between the timing of discontinuation of P2Y12 receptor antagonists and the incidence of the primary efficacy and safety outcomes. RESULTS: A total of 507 (median age 67 years; women 25%; STEMI 11%) patients treated with ticagrelor (19%) or clopidogrel (81%) for ACS met inclusion criteria. There was a significant association between the timing of CABG following P2Y12 discontinuation and a previous history of PCI, preoperative IABP or inotropes, STEMI as index event, and preoperative platelet levels. There was no association between the timing of CABG in relation to P2Y12receptor antagonist discontinuation and the specific P2Y12 receptor antagonist used (p=0.50). The unadjusted incidence of the primary efficacy outcome (11%) was comparable in relation to the timing of CABG following P2Y12 receptor antagonist discontinuation (p = 0.15). UDPB class ±2 bleeding rates were higher in patients undergoing CABG <48 hours (45%) and between 2 and <5 days (26%) compared to ±5 days (16%) following P2Y12 receptor antagonist discontinuation (p<0.0001). In our efficacy multivariable logistic regression model, the timing of discontinuation of P2Y12 receptor antagonist prior to CABG was not associated with the incidence of the primary efficacy outcome (figure); in our safety model, early CABG (<5 days following P2Y12 receptor antagonist discontinuation) was associated with increased incidence of UDPB ±2 bleeding (table). CONCLUSION: In patients with ACS requiring urgent or emergent CABG following P2Y12 receptor antagonist administration, early CABG following P2Y12 receptor antagonist discontinuation is associated with increased rates of bleeding of at least moderate severity but comparable rates of ischemic events. (Figure presented).
