N2  - BACKGROUND: The Canadian Cardiovascular Society (CCS) antiplatelet guidelines recommend the use of clopidogrel with acetylsalicylic acid (ASA; Aspirin™) for ST-segment elevation myocardial infarction (STEMI) patients managed with fibrinolytic therapy. In contrast, preferential use of the novel adenosine diphosphate receptor P2Y12 inhibitors (P2Y12ris) ticagrelor or prasugrel, is recommended in STEMI patients undergoing primary percutaneous coronary intervention (PCI). Thus, there is limited data on the safety and efficacy of the novel P2Y12ris within 24 hours of fibrinolysis. METHODS: Accordingly, we studied 1,328 STEMI patients from 26 Canadian PCI-capable hospitals (December 2011eOctober 2014) as part of the Canadian Observational Antiplatelet sTudy (COAPT), a prospective, observational, longitudinal study. Those who received fibrinolysis as the primary mode of reperfusion were divided into three groups: (1) started and then maintained clopidogrel AND underwent PCI ≤24 hours of fibrinolysis; (2) started on clopidogrel and switched to a novel ADPri AND underwent PCI ≤24 hours of fibrinolysis; and (3) on any ADPri AND underwent PCI >24 hours post-lysis; patients started and maintained on a novel P2Y12ri who underwent PCI within 24 hours of lysis (n=10) were excluded. Baseline characteristics and in-hospital and 15-month major adverse cardiac events (MACE) and patientreported bleeding of these three groups were compared. RESULTS: At index hospitalization, 261 of STEMI patients (19.7%) were treated with fibrinolysis as the initial reperfusion strategy, with 188 (72.0%) undergoing PCI within 24 hours of fibrinolysis. Baseline characteristics, oral antiplatelet therapy, MACE (death, MI, stroke, unplanned revascularization, stent thrombosis, cardiogenic shock and new or worsening congestive heart failure) and bleeding are summarized in the Table. 148 (56.7%) were started and maintained on clopidogrel and underwent early PCI, 40 (15.3%) were started on clopidogrel but switched to a novel P2Y12ri and underwent early PCI, and 73 (28.0%) were started on any P2Y12ri and underwent PCI >24 hours after lysis. CONCLUSIONS: 1 in 5 Canadian patients presenting with STEMI were initially treated with fibrinolysis. Consistent with CCS guidelines, 94% received clopidogrel as initial P2Y12ri therapy. While 21% were subsequently switched to a novel P2Y12ri within 24 hours of lysis and PCI, unadjusted MACE and bleeding rates were numerically lower when compared to those who started and were maintained on clopidogrel, albeit based upon a relatively small number of events. Thus, a randomized clinical trial comparing novel P2Y12ris to clopidogrel within the first 24 hours of lysis appears warranted. (Table Presented).
