N2  - BACKGROUND: A significant number of patients with acute coronary syndromes (ACS) treated upfront with dual antiplatelet therapy (DAPT) subsequently require emergent or urgent coronary artery bypass grafting surgery (CABG). Clinical outcomes following CABG in relation to the choice of P2Y12 receptor antagonists have not been well studied. METHODS: We retrospectively identified patients with ACS undergoing urgent or emergent CABG after treatment with clopidogrel or ticagrelor. We then examined clinical outcomes after stratifying patients based on the specific receptor antagonist used (clopidogrel versus ticagrelor), followed by substratification into three pre-specified time intervals from P2Y12 receptor antagonist discontinuation to CABG: <48 hours, 48 hours to <5 days, and ≥5 days. The primary efficacy outcome for this analysis was the composite of death, reinfarction, need for revascularization, or stroke; the primary safety outcome was bleeding of at least moderate severity as defined by a Universal Definition of Perioperative Bleeding (UDPB) class ≥ 2. We performed two multivariable logistic regression analyses to examine the relationship between choice of P2Y12 receptor antagonists and the incidence of the primary efficacy and safety outcomes. RESULTS: A total of 507 (median age 67 years; women 25%; STEMI 11%) patients treated with ticagrelor (19%) or clopidogrel (81%) for ACS met inclusion criteria. Compared to clopidogrel, ticagrelor use was more common in women, current smokers, patients with STEMI, and patients who were treated preoperatively with an IABP. There was no association between the timing of CABG in relation to P2Y12 receptor antagonist discontinuation and the specific P2Y12 receptor antagonist used (p=0.50). The unadjusted incidence of the primary efficacy outcome (11%) was comparable (p = 0.53) in patients receiving clopidogrel (12%) versus ticagrelor (10%) in the overall cohort (Table) and within each time-to-CABG stratum (Figure). UDPB class ≥2 bleeding was also comparable (p = 0.75) in patients treated with clopidogrel (25%) versus ticagrelor (26%) in the overall cohort (Table), and within each time-to-CABG stratum (Figure). In our efficacy multivariable logistic regression model, the use of ticagrelor compared to clopidogrel was not independently associated with the odds of the primary efficacy (OR 0.87, 95% CI 0.40-1.89, p = 0.72) or safety outcomes (OR 0.91, 95% CI 0.53-1.57, p = 0.74). CONCLUSION: In patients with ACS requiring urgent or emergent CABG following P2Y12 receptor antagonist administration, the choice of P2Y12 receptor antagonist (clopidogrel versus ticagrelor) was not associated with differential rates of ischemic or bleeding events. (Figure Presented).
