N2  - Background: Clopidogrel is commonly used in secondary prevention of ischemic stroke. Cytochrome P 450 system (CYP) is responsible for its metabolism which is involved in formation of its active metabolite. Influences on this system and end target of clopidogrel may modulate its effects. Objective: To study single nucleotide polymorphisms (SNPs) of CYP2C19, CYP2C9 and P2Y12 (target) gene and plasma adipokines (Leptin and Adiponectin) levels Methods: Ischemic stroke patients within 3 months were prospectively recruited from inpatient/outpatient of PGIMER after 1 week of clopidogrel (75 mg/day). Patients with cardioembolic strokes were exclusion. Patients were classified into 2 groups-group-I (clopidogrel alone) and group-II (clopidogrel plus aspirin). Clopidogrel effect was carried out by impedance based method on Chrono-Log platelet aggregometer (Model 700, USA) and matched with healthy controls. Impedance value of >5 ohm (Ω) was considered clopidogrel non-responder. Genotyping was carried out for SNPs of CYP2C19 [∗2, ∗3, ∗4 -Loss of function (LOF)], CYP2C9∗2 and P2Y12 genes using TaqMan based real-time PCR. Plasm levels of adipokines were measured using Enzyme-linked immunosorbent assay (ELISA). Results and Conclusion: 128 patients were recruited. Median age was 56.5 and 78% were males with Group-I (33.5%) and Group-II (66.5%). 52.3% were non-responders (group-I- 31% and group-II-69 %; p=0. 572). 54 % patients were carriers of at least one CYP2C19∗2 LOF allele comprising 61% of clopidogrel non-responders (p=0.037). Correlation between platelet aggregation and leptin/adiponectin levels in either group were non-significant and needs further study to ascertain role of non-genetic factors.
