N2  - Background: The genetic polymorphism in clopidogrel metabolism has a significant role in the recurrence of atherothrombotic events such as acute cerebrovascular accidents or acute myocardial infarction or acute coronary syndrome in patients on clopidogrel prophylaxis. The response to clopidogrel treatment varies inter-individually and inter-ethnically. Aims: The major genetic variants (SNPs) associated with CYP2C19 are CYP2C19-2, CYP2C19-3 and CYP2C19-17, of which CYP2C19-2 is common in Asian population. This study targets to analyze these SNPs in patients on clopidogrel therapy. Methodology: The SNPs associated with CYP2C19 were studied among 24 patients by TaqMan genotyping (qPCR) after genomic DNA isolation from peripheral blood. Results: Of these 24 patients, 9 patients (9/24)(37.5%) had recurrent atherothrombotic events and 15 patients (15/24)(62.5%) had no manifestations of recurrence. Among the 24 patients, 17 patients (17/ 24)(70.83%) were found to be heterozygous (AG instead of GG) for CYP2C19-2 variant and are predicted to be intermediate metabolisers of clopidogrel; 5 patients (5/24)(20.83%) were found to be homozygous to the variant CYP2C19-2 (AA instead of GG) and are poor metabolisers of clopidogrel; 2 patients (2/24)(8.3%) are normal metabolisers (GG) of clopidogrel. Conclusion: There is a high prevalence of CYP2C19-2 variant in our study population. Therefore, alternate anti-platelet therapy is moderately recommended in intermediate metabolisers and strongly recommended in poor metabolisers in order to prevent recurrent atherothrombotic events.
