N2  - Background: Antiplatelet resistance is one of the urgent issues in current stroke care. One-third to one-half of patients who experience a recurrent stroke are already on antiplatelet medications. We studied resistance to aspirin and clopidogrel in Indian stroke patients and its association with gene polymorphisms. Method: Platelet function testing by light transmission aggregometry was performed on 60 patients with ischemic stroke who were stable on dual antiplatelet therapy (clopidogrel 75 mg OD and aspirin 75 mg OD) along with 60 controls. Aspirin resistance was considered as mean platelet aggregation ≤70% with 10 mM ADP and ≤20% with 0.75mM arachidonic acid. Clopidogrel resistance was defined as <10% decrease from the baseline in platelet aggregation in response to ADP 10 mM and semiresponse as <30% decrease from the baseline. Polymorphisms CYP2C19∗2 and PLA1/A2 were genotyped by PCR-RFLP. Results: We found 65% (39/60) patients with inadequate response to clopidogrel [23.3% (14/60) resistant and 41.6% (25/60) semi-responders] and 5% (3/60) patients with inadequate response to aspirin [1.6% (1/60) resistant and 3.33% (2/60) semi-responder]. The frequency of CYP2C19∗2 mutant genotype was significantly higher in clopidogrel non-responders compared to responders (p<0.001). Clopidogrel nonresponsiveness was much higher in cardio embolic and small vessel stroke. No significant correlation was found between stroke risk factors and clopidogrel non-responsiveness, but higher clopidogrel non-responsiveness was found in smokers and alcoholics. Conclusions: Unlike aspirin, a high proportion of non-responders to clopidogrel were identified. In an interim analysis on 60 Indian patients, a significant association was found between CYP2C19∗2 and clopidogrel non-responsiveness.
