N2  - Background: Treatment of acute coronary syndromes (ACS) has been modified in recent years by the introduction of more potent P2Y12 inhibitors, such as prasugrel and ticagrelor. European Guidelines recommend to treat ACS patients with novel antiplatelets as first choice. In the real world, few data are available on the adherence to these recommendations, and on the follow-up of ACS in terms of thrombotic and bleeding complications with respect to antithrombotic therapy. Finally, the high prevalence of atrial fibrillation and other conditions requiring oral anticoagulant therapy makes more difficult in the real world the association of antithrombotic therapies. Methods: Participants to the START-ANTIPLATELETS, A multicenter, observational, prospective, non-interventional register, enroll all patients meeting predefined eligibility criteria (ACS); patients' data, including treatment, underlying conditions and type of antiplatelet, are collected on the register web-site. A 6-and 12-month follow-up is included. Moreover, all causes of temporary or definitive interruption of dual antiplatelet treatment are recorded. results 927 patients were registered (677 M/age:67±12.5y). Diagnosis included: 439 STEMI (47%); 367 NSTEMI (39.5%) and 120 UA (12.9%). 759 (81.8%) patients underwent PCI, of which 709 with stenting (328 DES); 147 (15.8%) were medically treated and 26 (3.1%) underwent coronary artery bypass graft surgery. Hypertension was present in 636 (68.6%); smoking habit in 433 (46.7%); hypercholesterolemia in 462 (49.8%); diabetes in 251 (27%) and family history of CAD in 260 (28%). At discharge antithrombotic therapy included: aspirin in 908 (97.9%; all on 100 mg/day except 2 on 75 mg/day and 7 on 300 mg/die); exposure to clopidogrel, prasugrel and ticagrelor occurred in 307 (33%), 118 (12.7%) and 427 (46%), respectively. 61 (6.5%) patients were treated with triple antithrombotic therapy: 49 with VKA+aspirin+clopidogrel; 10 with dabigatran+aspirin+clopidogrel and 2 with apixaban+aspirin+clopidogrel. 271 (29%) patients completed the 6-month follow-up: 12 (4.4%) died; 11 (4.1%) experienced cardiovascular death (7.6% on clopidogrel; 2% on prasugrel; none on ticagrelor). 2 (0.7%) patients experienced major bleeding (intracranial) (1 on prasugrel; 1 on clopidogrel) with subsequent death. 10 (3.6%) patients experienced minor bleedings (no differences among clopidogrel, ticagrelor and prasugrel). MACE were observed in 13 patients: 11 underwent urgent revascularization (7.8% on prasugrel; 3% on clopidogrel; 3% on ticagrelor); 1 ischemic stroke (on clopidogrel); 1 peripheral embolism (on clopidogrel). Conclusions: We expect to complete patients' follow-up in few months, so that the START-ANTIPLATELETS Register will allow us to enhance our understanding of the risk-benefits of the different antithrombotic strategies in Italian ACS patients.
