N2  - Background: Increased platelet inhibition by prasugrel or ticagrelor has been shown to result in reduced ischemic events along with greater bleeding hazards in patients with acute coronary syndromes (ACS) treated with percutaneous coronary intervention (PCI). Yet, at initiation of this study, there was limited actual data with the use of prasugrel in routine practice. We evaluated 1-year outcomes of a strategy with routine prasugrel with selective clopidogrel treatment in hospital survivors after ACS followed by PCI in a large-scale registry. Methods: The Rijnmond Collective Cardiology Research (CCR) registry is a prospective, observational study with enrollment of 4,258 PCI-treated ACS patients with 1-year follow-up in the larger region of Rotterdam, the Netherlands. Based on implemented hospital protocols, patients received prasugrel as first choice antiplatelet agent, with selective use of clopidogrel for increased risk patients (previous TIA or CVA, weight <60kg, age ≥75 years), including pretreatment before arrival at cathlab either pre-hospital (ambulance), in referring hospital or in-hospital (emergency room or coronary care unit), whenever possible. All events were validated by an independent clinical endpoint committee. Results: One hundred fourteen of the 4,258 died during admission, due to index event related mortality, not related to any bleedings. A total number of 2679 patients (65% of the remaining 4144) received prasugrel after the index event, 1420 (34%) received clopidogrel, only 45 (1%) received no P2Y12-inhibitor. 44% of the patients presented with ST-elevation myocardial infarction, 72% were men, and mean age was 63.7±12.2 years. Age above 75 year was the most frequent bleeding risk factor (presence: 21.4%, followed by previous stroke of TIA: 7.2% and low body weight: 3.1%). At 1 year, a composite of all-cause death and myocardial infarction, occurred in 143 (3.5%) of hospital survivors. All-cause death occurred in 82 (2.0%), myocardial infarction in 67 (1.6%), target-vessel revascularization in 147 (3.6%), stent thrombosis in 35 (0.8%), and stroke in 49 (1.2%). With careful selection of patients for most potent platelet inhibition the primary safety endpoint of all TIMI major bleeding episodes of 1.4% in the prasugrel treated patients was not increased vs our clopidogrel treated patients (TIMI Major bleeds of 2.5%) Conclusions: Final results from the CCR study show that a standardized protocol to optimize the use of most effective platelet inhibition in ACS resulted in a high adoption rate with low rates of ischemic events, including overall mortality in hospital survivors, and no excess rates of major bleeding in routine practice up to 1 year post-PCI.
