N2  - Background: Indications for the three main P2Y12 receptor antagonists differ. Prasugrel is indicated in patients with acute coronary syndrome (ACS) treated with percutaneous coronary intervention, whereas ticagrelor and clopidogrel have broad ACS indications as part of dual antiplatelet therapy with acetylsalicylic acid (ASA). Clopidogrel is also indicated for stroke, peripheral artery disease and stable coronary heart disease, where concomitant ASA use is not required. Purpose: The aim of this retrospective cohort study was to describe characteristics and safety outcomes in patients receiving a first prescription of clopidogrel, prasugrel or ticagrelor. Methods: Patients 20 to 84 years old who were dispensed clopidogrel, prasugrel or ticagrelor for the first time between 1 June 2011 and 31 December 2012 were identified from the Swedish Prescribed Drug Register (PDR). Only patients without a history of P2Y12 receptor antagonist use (naïve users) were included. Concomitant medications were identified from the PDR and diagnoses were identified from the Swedish National Patient Register. Patients were followed from the first dispensing date until the first occurrence of a safety outcome, the end of the follow-up period (31 December 2013), their 85th birthday or death. Safety outcomes included hospitalizations and outpatient visits (not primary care) for bleeding, bradyarrhythmias, cardiac arrest, heart failure, acute renal failure, acute liver injury, dyspnoea, syncope or gout. Safety signals were evaluated using crude incidence rates (CIRs). Results: The median duration of first continuous use was 11 months for clopidogrel (n=41,693), 12 months for prasugrel (n=991) and 10.5 months for ticagrelor (n=6,186). Comorbid cardiovascular disease was more common in patients using ticagrelor (99%) and prasugrel (96%) than in those using clopidogrel (54%), whereas cerebrovascular disease was more common in patients using clopidogrel (34% vs. 4% [ticagrelor and prasugrel]). Concomitant ASA use was seen in 98%, 97% and 76% of patients using ticagrelor, prasugrel and clopidogrel, respectively. In patients with a history of myocardial infarction (MI/ACS - a proxy for patients with a similar treatment indication) the CIR of dyspnoea was higher in ticagrelor users (26.3; 95% confidence interval [CI]: 22.1-31.0) than in users of clopidogrel (15.6; 95% CI: 14.2-17.6) or prasugrel (9.4; 95% CI: 4.3-17.8). A similar pattern was seen in patients with MI and/or chronic obstructive pulmonary disease (COPD). No other clear differences in safety outcomes were seen across the cohorts. Conclusions: Characteristics of naïve users of clopidogrel, prasugrel and ticagrelor seem to reflect the registered indications for these drugs. In patients with a history of MI and/or COPD there was a higher CIR of dyspnoea for ticagrelor users than for clopidogrel and prasugrel users. No other safety signals were observed.
