N2  - Introduction: Dual anti-platelet therapy is routinely used in patients with acute coronary syndromes (ACS). However, it is unclear whether the benefit of P2Y12 inhibitors varies depending on sex. Purpose: To perform a systematic review and meta-analysis of P2Y12 blockade added to aspirin therapy in the management of ACS with respect to sex. Methods: Electronic databases (MEDLINE and Embase) were searched for papers published 1946-2014; plus abstracts from major cardiology conferences. Only randomised controlled trials of P2Y12 inhibitors in the setting of ACS were included that had clinical outcomes by sex. 7 eligible studies were identified and appraised by two independent authors. 4 studies compared clopidogrel to control; 2 compared prasugrel to clopidogrel; 1 compared ticagrelor to clopidogrel. Combined, these studies recruited 89,892 patients. Risk ratios (RR) and 95% confidence intervals (CI) were calculated for a composite endpoint of cardiovascular death, myocardial infarction or stroke (MACE), and a safety endpoint of major bleeding for each sex. Indirect comparison analysis was performed to statistically compare ticagrelor against prasugrel. Results: Compared to aspirin alone, clopidogrel was superior at reducing MACE with a RR of 0.92 (CI: 0.85-0.99, p=0.02) and 0.84 (CI: 0.75-0.92, p=0.003) respectively for women and men. Compared to clopidogrel, ticagrelor reduced the risk of MACE in both women and men (RR of 0.84 (95% CI: 0.72-0.97 p=0.02) and 0.83 (95% CI: 0.75-0.92, p=0.0003) respectively). Prasugrel was shown to benefit only men, RR of 0.80 (95% CI:0.73-0.88, p<0.00001) and not women, RR of 0.93 (95% CI:0.82-1.06). When indirectly comparing ticagrelor to prasugrel, no statistically significant difference was observed in either women or men, with a RR of 0.90 (95% CI:0.74-1.10) and 1.05 (95% CI:0.91-1.20) respectively. Review of bleeding profile showed that when compared to placebo, clopidogrel was associated with a significant higher rate of major bleeding in women alone. However, neither sex demonstrated an increased bleeding risk when on ticagrelor versus clopidogrel. Sex specific data for bleeding was not available for prasugrel. (Figure Presented) Conclusions: In summary, P2Y12 inhibitors reduce MACE in women and men. Ticagrelor was superior to clopidogrel in both sexes, but prasugrel showed a statistically significant benefit only in men. However indirect comparison does not demonstrate superiority of ticagrelor over prasugrel in either women or men.
