N2  - Background: P2Y12-inhibitor initiation using clopidogrel loading dose (LD) vs no loading dose (NLD) provides more rapid and potent inhibition of platelet activation and reduced risk of ischaemic events following coronary stenting Purpose: Evaluate the effect of clopidogrel LD on outcomes in patients (pts) undergoing transcatheter aortic valve implantation (TAVI) Methods: The BRAVO-3 trial randomized 802 pts with severe aortic stenosis undergoing transfemoral TAVI to intra-procedure anticoagulation with bivalirudin or heparin; random treatment assignment did not affect 48-h or 30-day outcomes Major adverse cardiac events (MACE) were defined as the composite of death myocardial infarction or stroke, and net adverse cardiac events (NACE) as MACE or BARC ≥3b bleeding Results: Patients were stratified according to receiving clopidogrel LD (n=294 36.6%) or NLD (n=508, 63.4%) before TAVI. Characteristics of both groups were similar, but prior CABG was more common and atrial fibrillation less common in the LD group. Among pts on a LD, 83/294 (28.2%) were on maintenance vs 167/505 (33.1%) in the NLD group. In total, 74.6% (n=595) were recommended a daily dose until 30 days. Comparison between bivalirudin and heparin according to clopidogrel LD did not show significant differences in outcomes. Conclusions: In pts undergoing TAVI, use of clopidogrel LD is associated with increased vascular complications without significant decreased ischaemic events. (Figure Presented).
