N2  - Background: Long-term efficacy and safety data in “real life” ACS-PCI cohort treated with clopidogrel, prasugrel or ticagrelor are scarce. Methods: In a prospective, multicenter, observational study 2047 patients were recruited and were followed-up until 1 year for major adverse cardiovascular events (MACE, composite of death, non fatal myocardial infarction, urgent revascularization and stroke) and bleeding events [Bleeding Academic Research Consortium (BARC) classification]. Results: Exposure to clopidogrel, prasugrel and ticagrelor at PCI hospital occurred in 959, 363 and 717 patients respectively. After adjustment, MACE rate was lower in prasugrel (4.4%) vs clopidogrel (10.1%) treated patients [HR, 95% CI, 0.53 (0.30-0.93), p=0.03], though not significantly different between ticagrelor (6.8%) and clopidogrel groups [HR (95% CI) 0.80 (0.56-1.15), p=0.2]. BARC any type bleeding was more frequent in prasugrel (51.2%) vs clopidogrel (37.6%) [HR (95% CI) 1.62 (1.34-1.95)] and in ticagrelor (56.9%) vs clopidogrel treated patients [HR (95% CI) 1.82 (1.57-2.11)], p<0.001. BARC type ≥3 bleeding did not differ significantly among the 3 P2Y12 receptor antagonists used. After adjustment, death rate was reduced by novel agents vs clopidogrel, 2.9% vs 6.2%, p=0.03. Conclusions: In real-word ACS/PCI patients, prasugrel seems to offer better antiischemic protection than clopidogrel, while use of both novel agents is accompanied by more frequent bleeding events.
