N2  - Background: The association between P2Y12 platelet receptor inhibition with clopidogrel and risk for ischemic cerebrovascular events in patients undergoing percutaneous coronary intervention (PCI) with drug-eluting stents (DES) is unclear. Purpose: We investigated the association between platelet reactivity on clopidogrel and the risk of ischemic stroke (IS) after successful DES PCI. Methods: Patients from the ADAPT-DES study were categorized by IS occurrence at 2 years. Platelet reactivity on clopidogrel was assessed by means of VerifyNow point-of-care assay after successful PCI. We estimated the incidence of stroke in patients with or without platelet reactive units (PRU) >208 by the Kaplan-Meier method. We estimated the adjusted association between IS and PRU >208 by Cox proportional hazards regression and identified the PRU cutoff associated with the best sensitivity and specificity (highest Youden index) by receiver operating curve analysis (ROC). The association between IS and 2-year all-cause mortality was estimated by entering IS as a time-varying covariate in a multivariable Cox-regression model. Results: Out of 8,582 patients enrolled in ADAPT-DES study, 68 had an IS. Patients who had a stroke were older and were affected by a greater number of cardiovascular and non-cardiovascular comorbidities. Two-year rates of stroke in patients with or without PRU >208 are illustrated in Figure. By multivariable Cox regression analysis, PRU >208 was independently associated with increased risk of IS (adjusted hazard ratio [HR]: 1.81; 95% confidence interval [CI]: 1.08-3.04; p=0.03). ROC analysis identified a PRU >202 as the best cutoff for IS (66% sensitivity and 55% specificity). The association of PRU >202 was similar in magnitude to that of PRU >208 (Adjusted HR: 1.96; 95% CI: 1.15-3.33; p=0.01). Following adjustment for baseline confounders IS was strongly associated with 2-year allcause mortality (adjusted HR: 4.16; 95% CI: 1.95-8.87; p<0.0001). Conclusions: Clopidogrel hypo-responsiveness is independently associated with increased risk of IS after successful DES PCI. The anti-ischemic benefits of enhanced and/or prolonged platelet inhibition possibly extend beyond the coronary vasculature to the cerebral arterial system. (Figure Presented).
