N2  - Introduction: According to the most recent US and European PCI guidelines, P2Y12 receptor inhibitor therapy is not generally recommended beyond 1 year in patients previously underwent PCI with drug-eluting stents(DES). On the contrary, in clinical practice, there are still unmet needs to overcome residual ischemic burden by prolonged DAPT with clopidogrel on top of aspirin in high-risk patient subset. However, there are few studies assessing the risk of the patients on prolonged DAPT. Objectives: To evaluate the association between mid-term(at 1 year) on-clopidogrel PR in patients with DES and long-term adverse events. Methods: We measured platelet reactivity (PR) by P2Y12 reaction unit(PRU) values derived from VerifyNow P2Y12 assay (Accriva, USA) at 1 year after index PCI. The high on-clopidogrel platelet reactivity (HPR) was defined as PRU>208. The ischemic and bleeding events were compared according to HPR and non-HPR status at mid-term. The ischemic events composed of death, myocardial infarction(MI), stroke or transient ischemic attack(TIA). The bleeding event was defined as any bleeding according to Bleeding Academic Research Consortium(BARC) classification. Baseline characteristics, laboratory parameters and angiographic data are also analyzed. Results: A total of 344 patients(mean age 61.6 years, M:F=250:94) previously underwent DES-based PCI 1 year ago has been entered to this study. Duration of event follow-up was 47.2 months. There showed no difference in the rate of death and stroke/TIA between HPR and non-HPR groups(0.5% vs 0%, p>0.99; 0.5% vs 1.5%; p=0.58, respectively). However, there was higher incidence of MI in HPR group as compared with non-HPR group(4.2% vs. 1.5%, p=0.04). On the contrary, the incidence of any-BARC bleeding was lower in HPR group than non-HPR group(0.5% vs. 6.9%, p<0.01). Conclusion: The mid-term platelet reactivity showed association with long-term ischemic and bleeding event, suggesting the possibility of its use as a prognostic marker in patients on prolonged DAPT. Further large-scale investigation is warranted.
