N2  - Aims: Potent P2Y12 inhibitors may offer enhanced benefit in complex percutaneous coronary intervention (PCI). We sought to examine 1-year outcomes with prasugrel versus clopidogrel in patients undergoing complex PCI from the PROMETHEUS study. Methods and results: PROMETHEUS was a retrospective multicentre observational study comparing clopidogrel and prasugrel use in patients undergoing PCI for acute coronary syndrome (n=19,914; 76% complex, 24% non-complex PCI). We compared 1-year major adverse cardiac events (MACE, a composite of death, myocardial infarction, stroke or unplanned revascularisation) between clopidogreland prasugrel-treated patients stratified by complexity of PCI. Complex PCI was defined by PCI of the left main, bifurcation lesion, B2/C type lesion, moderately-severely calcified lesion or total stent length ≥30 mm. Data were adjusted for MACE using propensity score adjustment methods. Of the total cohort, prasugrel was prescribed to 21% patients undergoing complex PCI and to 19% undergoing non-complex PCI. Analogously, clopidogrel was prescribed to 79% of complex PCI and to 81% of non-complex PCI patients. In both complex and non-complex PCI groups, prasugrel-treated patients were younger, and there were fewer women and fewer clinical comorbidities including diabetes, hypertension, prior myocardial infarction and PCI than among clopidogrel-treated patients. Irrespective of thienopyridine prescribed, the complex PCI group had a high proportion of multivessel disease, B2/C type lesions and bifurcation PCI. However, prasugrel-treated complex PCI patients had a lower prevalence of calcification, left main PCI and greater use of second-generation DES and greater total stent lengths. In complex PCI patients, 1-year MACE was 12.7% with prasugrel vs. 22.0% with clopidogrel. In the non-complex PCI patients, 1-year MACE was 10.0% with prasugrel vs. 17.0% with clopidogrel. After propensity adjustment, the benefit of prasugrel over clopidogrel was preserved in complex PCI patients (HR 0.86, 95% CI: 0.76-0.97), but was attenuated in those undergoing non-complex PCI (HR 0.90, 95% CI: 0.70-1.17), p-interaction 0.94. Bleeding occurred in 3.4% vs. 5.0% of complex PCI patients (p<0.001) with prasugrel versus clopidogrel and in 2.1% vs. 4.1% of non-complex PCI patients (p <0.09). Conclusions: Compared with clopidogrel use, prasugrel provided significant benefit in patients undergoing complex PCI in a propensity-adjusted analysis.
