N2  - Aims: To investigate effect of CYP2C19 genetic variants on antiplatelet therapy in Chinese patients with acute myocardial infarction after primary PCI, and compare antiplatelet effect between clopidogrel and ticagrelor in patients with genetic mutations. Methods and results: Patients (N=347) with acute myocardial infarction after primary PCI were enrolled. All patients received a loading and maintenance dose of dual antiplatelet therapy (aspirin+clopidogrel/ticagrelor). In the clopidogrel group there were 221 cases, and 126 in the ticagrelor group. Two candidate single nucleotide polymorphisms (636 and 681 loci) of the CYP2C19 gene were detected by real-time fluorescence quantitative PCR. All cases were divided into wild type, heterozygous and homozygous according to allele carrying condition. The effect of the antiplatelet was assessed by thrombelastography platelet mapping assay 24-48 hours after primary PCI. All patients were followed up for 30 days and 6 months after discharge. Baseline data were matched between the two groups. For the clopidogrel group, the fast metabolic type (non-carriers, two loci were both wild type) included 93 cases (42.1%). The mid metabolic type (either of two loci was heterozygous) and the slow metabolic type (one locus was homozygous or both of the two loci were heterozygous) defined as carriers totalled 128 cases (57.9%), including 29 homozygous cases (13.1%). For the ticagrelor group, non-carriers were 48 cases (38.1%), and carriers were 78 cases (61.9%), including 17 homozygous cases (13.5%). There was no significant difference between the two groups of CYP2C19 genotype frequency. Comparing the clopidogrel and ticagrelor groups, the platelet function of TEGADP was 63.08±27.78 vs. 78.08±25.62, p<0.0001, MAADP was 31.35±15.29 vs. 24.14±13.74, p<0.0001. For non-carriers, TEGADP was 68.16±25.17 vs. 82.79±22.45, p=0.002, MAADP was 29.47±14.39 vs .23.03±13.03, p=0.008; for carriers, TEGADP were 59.37±29.06 vs. 75.15±27.15, p<0.0001, MAADP was 32.71±15.83 vs. 24.82±14.74, p=0.001. Compared with the clopidogrel group, the percentage inhibition of platelet aggregation of the ticagrelor group was significantly higher. In the comparison of antiplatelet effect among the three kinds of metabolic type in the clopidogrel group (fast, mid and slow), TEGADP were 68.16±25.17 vs. 58.01±29.29 vs. 63.75±28.34, p=0.047. For the ticagrelor group, TEGADP were 82.79±22.45 vs. 76.71±24.23 vs. 69.04±36.98, p=0.188. Incidence of myocardial infarction within 30 days after discharge in the clopidogrel group and the ticagrelor group was, respectively, 1.4% vs. 0.8%, p=0.540, target lesion revascularisation (TLR) 0.9% vs. 0.8%, p=0.701, death 1.4% vs. 1.6%, p=0.598, stroke 0.5% vs. 0.8%, p=0.595, bleeding 1.4% vs. 0, p=0.257. At 6-month follow-up, the incidence of myocardial infarction was 0.5% vs. 0, p=0.637, TLR 0.5% vs. 0, p=0.637, death 0 vs. 0.8%, p=0.363. There was no obvious difference between the two groups in the incidence of MACE, bleeding and stroke at 30-day and 6-month follow-up. According to the different genotypes, MACE, haemorrhage and stroke incidence also showed no significant difference between the two groups. Conclusions: Insufficiency of the antiplatelet effect of clopidogrel may relate to CYP2C19 gene mutations. The antiplatelet effect of ticagrelor is much stronger than clopidogrel, and this effect is not affected by CYP2C19 gene mutations. There is no obvious difference between the incidence of clinical events.
