N2  - Aims: Differences in response to prasugrel and ticagrelor in the setting of acute coronary syndromes (ACS) in a population from east Asia vs. Europe have not yet been investigated. Methods and results: This is a sub-analysis of the “Bleeding complications in a Multicenter international registry of patients discharged after an Acute Coronary Syndrome (BleeMACS)”. All the patients admitted for ACS treated with aspirin who underwent PCI from January 2012 to December 2014 have been included and stratified according to their provenance from Europe vs. east Asia (China and Japan). We compared the adjusted rate of in-hospital and 1-year serious bleeding between these patients as a safety endpoint and in-hospital and 1-year death/re-infarction as an effectiveness endpoint of the new P2Y12r inhibitors. Data of 10,004 patients attended in Europe and 2,332 patients in East Asia (China: 2 centres, 1376 patients; Japan: 3 centres, 956 patients) were collected. At baseline prior stroke (6% vs. 9%, p<0.001, respectively), malignancy (7% vs. 5%, p=0.03) and type of ACS (59% vs. 71% STEMI, 11% vs. 21% unstable angina) were significantly different between groups. Clopidogrel (86% vs. 91%, p<0.001) and ticagrelor (5% vs. 6%, p=0.01) were slightly less used in patients from european centres compared to those managed in the east Asian hospitals, in favour of prasugrel (7% vs 0%, p<0.001). In-hospital bleeding were significantly higher in patients treated in Europe comparing to those in east Asian countries (8% vs. 4%, p<0.001), but at 1 year no difference was found (3% vs. 3%, p=0.84). The between-group incidence of in-hospital death/re-infarction did not differ significantly (3% vs. 2% respectively, p=0.78), while it was significantly higher at 1-year in the European centres (9% vs. 5%, p<0.001). At the multivariate analysis, in both sub-groups, ticagrelor decreases the risk of MACE death/re-infarction (Europe: HR 0.5, CI 0.3-0.9; East Asia: HR 0.5, CI 0.2-0.9), despite a higher risk of bleeding only in Caucasians (HR 1.7, CI 1.1-2.6). In contrast, prasugrel reduce death/re-infarction (HR 0.4, CI 0.2-0.6), without increasing of bleeding (HR 0.9, CI 0.5-1.3). Conclusions: In the setting of the ACS, the new antiplatelets drugs seem to be safe and efficacious at midterm follow-up independently from the geographic area. Among the P2Y12i available, prasugrel seems to have the best risk-benefit, while ticagrelor appears safer in Asian patients.
