N2  - Aims: P2Y12 inhibitors may be interrupted prematurely for various reasons after PCI for the culprit lesions of STEMI. The aim of this study was to assess the impact of premature discontinuation and/or disruption of prasugrel following primary PCI. Methods and results: Between September 2009 and January 2014, 1,716 patients with STEMI undergoing primary PCI were enrolled at 12 European and Swiss centres in the framework of a randomised trial and a large-scale registry. Clinical follow-up information was obtained at discharge, 30 days and one year. Reasons for prasugrel discontinuation (defined as planned and physician-driven prasugrel cessation) and disruption (defined as patient-driven or unplanned prasugrel cessation) were assessed. The risk for ischaemic outcomes was calculated using patients who remained on prasugrel throughout one year as reference. The primary endpoint was a composite of cardiac death, myocardial infarction, and stroke (MACCE). A total of 1,716 STEMI patients were included: 1,479 (86.2%) patients completed the prescribed type and duration of DAPT using prasugrel throughout one year; 64 (3.7%) patients prematurely discontinued prasugrel according to the discretion of the treating physician, 54 (3.1%) patients disrupted prasugrel intake; and the remaining 119 (6.9%) patients substituted prasugrel with clopidogrel. Compared to patients who continued prasugrel throughout one year, disruption of prasugrel was associated with an increased risk of MACCE (HR 2.85, 95% CI: 1.46-10.62, p=0.007). Conversely, discontinuation of prasugrel (HR 1.66, 95% CI: 0.61-4.53, p=0.32) or substitution of prasugrel with clopidogrel (HR 0.87, 95% CI: 0.35-2.15, p=0.76) was associated with similar risks of MACCE. Similarly, hazards for cardiac death (HR 6.98, 95% CI: 2.35-20.68, p<0.001), myocardial infarction (HR 3.00, 95% CI: 1.11-8.33, p=0.031), and stent thrombosis (HR 3.8, 95% CI: 1.17-12.57) were increased among patients who disrupted prasugrel, whereas no significant risk was observed among patients who discontinued prasugrel or substituted prasugrel with clopidogrel. Most frequent reasons for prasugrel discontinuation were oral anticoagulation with vitamin K antagonists (29.7%) or planned interventions (21.9%), and most frequent reasons for prasugrel disruption were patient initiative (37.0%), bleeding (22.2%), adverse effects (18.5%) or financial constraints (11.1%). Conclusions: Premature disruption but not discontinuation of prasugrel following primary PCI is associated with an increased risk of MACCE.
