N2  - Aims: In this report, we present the 1-year outcomes from the GReek AntiPlatElet Registry, which represent “real-life” experience incorporating the contemporary use of 3 oral P2Y12 receptor antagonists. Methods and results: In a prospective, observational, multicentre, cohort study, 2,047 patients were recruited and were followed up for 1 year for major adverse cardiovascular events (MACE, a composite of death, non-fatal myocardial infarction, urgent revascularisation and stroke) and bleeding events (Bleeding Academic Research Consortium [BARC] classification). Exposure to clopidogrel, prasugrel and ticagrelor at the PCI hospital occurred in 959, 363 and 717 patients, respectively. After adjustment, the MACE rate (primary outcome endpoint) was lower in prasugrel (4.4%) vs. clopidogrel (10.1%) treated patients (hazard ratio [HR] 0.55, 95% confidence interval [CI]: 0.32-0.96, p=0.035), though not significantly different between ticagrelor (6.8%) and clopidogrel groups (HR 0.79, 95% CI: 0.55-1.13, p=0.2). BARC any type bleeding (secondary outcome endpoint) was more frequent in prasugrel (51.2%) vs. clopidogrel (37.6%) (HR 1.61, 95% CI: 1.33-1.95) and in ticagrelor (56.9%) vs. clopidogrel treated patients (HR 1.80, 95% CI: 1.55-2.10), p<0.001 for both. BARC type ≥3 bleeding did not differ significantly among the 3 P2Y12 receptor antagonists used. After adjustment, the death rate was reduced by novel agents vs. clopidogrel, 2.9% vs. 6.2% (HR 0.55, 95% CI: 0.34-0.88), p=0.01. Conclusions: In real-word ACS patients subjected to PCI, prasugrel seems to offer better anti-ischaemic protection than clopidogrel, whilst use of both novel agents is accompanied by more frequent bleeding events.
