N2  - Background: Clopidogrel is an antiplatelet drug that requires biotransformation steps to its active metabolite via cytochromes P450 (CYP), particularly CYP2C19. Recently, nonresponsiveness is widely recognized and is related to raising rates of recurrent ischemic events. The response to clopidogrel therapy shows a wide interindividual variation with 5 to 30[percnt] of clopidogrel-treated patients exhibit low or no reactivity to clopidogrel, which is referred as clopidogrel resistance with high residual platelet aggregation. Objective: To elucidate the degree of genetic polymorphisms CYP2C19 (CYP2C19∗2, CYP2C19∗3) of key drug metabolizing enzymes on the antiplatelet effect of clopidogrel response in patients with acute Ischemic Stroke from Saudi Arabia. Methodology: 25 stroke Patients showing responders to clopidogrel therapy and 25 stroke patients showing non responders to clopidogrel monotherapy were recruited after getting their informed consent and genotyped for CYP2C19 ∗2, CYP2C19∗3 polymorphisms by Polymerase chain reaction-restriction fragment length polymorphisms (PCR-RFLP Method). Allele frequencies was derived from genotypic data and Platelet aggregation was measured using MEA on the Multiplateanalyser. Chi Square tests, P value, Odds ratio (ORs) and corresponding confidence intervals was calculated for each polymorphism. Results: The CYP2C19∗2 (681G>A), CYP2C19∗3 (636 G>A) polymorphism was seen to be in Hardy-Weinberg equilibrium and showed significant allelic and genotypic association between responders and non responders to clopidogrel (P<0.01). CYP2C19 ∗2 : Allelic Chi-square = 21.49, p =0.000036; Odds Ratio OR: 5.52 (2.42-12.83), Genotypic Chi-square = 10.27, p = 0.001 : Odds Ratio OR : 7.88 (1.78-9.73). CYP2C19 ∗3: Allelic chi-square: 11.66, p = 0.0006, Odds Ratio OR: 3.45 (1.57-7.70), Genotypic Chi-square = 4.37, p= 0.036: Odds Ratio: 3.69 (0.90-5.81). The Variant Allele (Homozygous and homozygous Mutant) showed significant influence on platelet inhibition and antiplatelet effect of clopidogrel in Ischemic Stroke . Conclusion: Our findings provide certain evidence on genetic effect of CYP2C19 on clopidogrel responsiveness in stroke patients from Saudi.
