N2  - Frail patients with coronary artery disease (CAD) on P2Y12 inhibitors are at risk of major bleeding. Bleeding complications are more prevalent in patients with low on-treatment platelet reactivity (PR). We aimed to assess the agreement between the VerifyNow P2Y12, Multiplate ADP and light transmission aggregometry (LTA) with 20 μM ADP when classifying frail CAD patients in PR-categories (low, optimal, high), using proposed therapeutic windows by a consensus paper (Tantry et al. [2013] Journal of the American College of Cardiology 24:2261) and to identify factors that influence agreement. Platelet function tests (PFTs) were performed within 6 months after percutaneous intervention in 145 consecutive patients, to determine safety and efficacy of P2Y12-inhibitors, according to the Tantry criteria. Frailty was defined as having ≥ 3 risk factors for bleeding. Agreement between PFT pairs was assessed by the quadratic weighted kappa method. Multiplate-VerifyNow agreed in 72 patients (50%), κ = 0.41; VerifyNow-LTA agreed in 76 patients (52%), κ = 0.36; LTA-Multiplate agreed in 64 patients (44%), κ = 0.20. Factors that significantly improved or reduced agreement differed per PFT pair and included drug use (prasugrel or ticagrelor vs. clopidogrel, aspirin, proton pump inhibitors), laboratory parameters (haemoglobin level, platelet count) and patient characteristics (previous stroke). Most of these factors also significantly influenced the unexplained variation between PR-measurements, partly explaining why these factors influenced agreement. In conclusion, classifying frail CAD patients in PR-categories according to the consensus paper resulted in slight to moderate agreement between PFTs, which was influenced by drug use, laboratory parameters and patient characteristics. PFTs with proposed therapeutic windows are not interchangeable and the choice of PFT can directly affect treatment strategy, which might even be more prominent in patients with factors influencing agreement. Clinical endpoints and preferably more studies comparing PFTs are needed to optimize the therapeutic windows.
