N2  - Aims: Platelet function is usually studied in freshly prepared whole blood or in PRP derived from it within two to four hours of obtaining the blood. PAMFix (Platelet Solutions Ltd, Nottingham, UK) is a fixative that was developed to stabilize the platelet biomarker P-selectin on activated platelets for at least 9 days. The aim here was to determine whether stabilization of this biomarker introduced the possibility of performing measurements of platelet function remotely where there is no access to special equipment or special technical expertise. Methods: Platelets in whole blood or PRP were stimulated with a variety of different platelet agonists following which PAMFix was added. The fixed blood samples were then transported to a central laboratory where the platelets were examined using flow cytometry. Results: 1. Levels of P-selectin on platelets in whole blood were determined at different times after platelet stimulation and also before and after transportation of fixed blood samples at ambient temperatures. Excellent stability was demonstrated, first up to 9 days, and later up to 28 days. 2. A particular use for PAMFix is as a component of various Platelet Kits for measurements of platelet function, including Aspirin Kits and P2Y12 Kits designed to determine the inhibitory effects of aspirin and P2Y12 antagonists respectively. Aspirin profoundly inhibited P-selectin expression in blood from over 95% of patients with coronary heart disease, however over half of patients on clopidogrel did not respond well to treatment. Prasugrel was significantly more effective than clopidogrel but only in patients without hypertension. Patients with cardiovascular disease who exhibit high residual P-selectin measured using the P2Y12 test were shown to have a significantly raised likelihood of a further thrombotic event (p=0.026). 3. P-selectin measurements on stored blood samples correlate well with other measures of platelet function, including light transmission aggregometry, whole blood aggregometry and assays of VASP phosphorylation. 4. PAMFix is also useful for assessing platelet function in stroke, for diagnosis in patients with platelet dysfunction, and for veterinary studies. Conclusions: There is now considerable experience of using activation markers, especially P-selectin, on stabilized platelets in stored, fixed, blood samples as a means of measuring platelet function. The approach is simple to use at the point that a blood sample is obtained. Fixation obviated the need for special equipment or technical expertise at the point of blood sampling.
