N2  - Platelets are key cells in maintaining hemostasis, but they are play important role in the pathogenesis atherothrombotic diseases are still the primary death reasons in the worldwide. Following endothelial damage, platelets adhere with the collagen in the subendothelium and aggregate upon their activation with different agonists (e.g. ADP, TXA2, 5HT, PAF, and thrombin). If this process in excessively activated, it leads to thrombotic disorder leading to vascular blockage. Antiplatelet drugs have a pivotal role in the prevention of myocardial infarction and stroke result from thrombotic occlusion and ischemic damage, and in decreasing the risk of death related to these diseases. There are several classes of antiplatelet drugs targeting main platelet receptors or its signaling pathways (e.g. COX-1 inhibitor, ADP receptor P2Y12 antagonists, αIIbβ3 integrin (GPIIb/IIIa) receptor antagonists, thrombin PAR-1 receptor antagonists, and phosphodiesterase inhibitors). An ideal antiplatelet drug should specifically inhibit thrombogenic platelet dependent mechanisms in vascular diseases without interfering with platelet function providing the normal hemostasis and also should be free from any major adverse effects. None of the currently developed drugs meet these criteria at present. The major adverse effects of current most widely used antiplatelet drugs are bleeding and bleeding risk limiting the benefits of these drugs. Furthermore, variability in response and resistance towards antiplatelet drugs such as aspirin and clopidogrel which are commonly used in therapy, limited efficacy, failure in oral applicability (e.g. GPIIb/IIIa antagonists) and necessity of monitoring dosage are the other problems to be solved in the future antiplatelet therapy regimen and accelerate the studies to find newer targets and to develop newer antiplatelet agents. Collagen-specific platelet glycoprotein VI (GPVI) receptor which is a member of immunoreceptor family and its signaling represents one of the attractive new targets for antiplatelet therapy without bleeding risk because it is only expressed on platelet and platelet precursor cells megakaryocytes. Also, compounds targeting GPVI will enable opportunity to prevent thrombosis at early phase because of that interaction between GPVI and collagen occurs at early phase of platelet adhesion and activation. In addition, targeting intracellular signaling downstream from G-protein-coupled receptors (GPCRs) with PI3Kβ and Gq are the novel strategies under investigation. Apart from the ongoing clinical research devoted to the most promising targets (e.g. PARs, GPVI, and GPIb-von Willebrand factor axis) or preclinical investigation on potential targets, the improvements in understanding of complex intracellular signaling mechanisms of platelets will provide an opportunity to discover novel compound which are more effective and with less off target effects in the future.
