N2  - Introduction: Increased risk of thrombo-occlusive events in clopidogreltreated individuals arising from genetically determined slow CYP2C19- mediated metabolism has been established in the context of ischaemic heart disease. Few studies have considered the ischaemic stroke (IS) population where current guidelines recommend clopidogrel monotherapy. Method: We undertook a Mendelian randomised observational cohort study in the GoDARTS bioresource to investigate the clinical impact of the CYP2C19∗2 variant in patients prescribed clopidogrel following an arterial thrombo-occlusive event. From ∼15,000 genotyped individuals 954 had a hospitalisation for any arterial thrombo-occlusive event within 6 months prior to their first community encashment for clopidogrel. The IS sub-population comprised 158 individuals. Results: During 6months follow-up there were 272 re-hospitalisations for an arterial thrombo-occlusive event or death. In a Cox regression model including age, sex, aspirin and proton pump inhibitor exposure, the 25% of individuals carrying the CYP2C19∗2 allele had an increased risk, HR 1.29 (95% CI 1.03-1.60) p = 0.025, compared to non carriers. In the IS population there were 26 events and the relative risk was greater, HR 2.27 (95% CI 1.29-4.00) p = 0.004. In a meta-analysis including a larger clinical study the overall HR was 2.28, 95% CI 1.53-3.38, p = 0.00004. Discussion: A quarter of IS patients treated with clopidogrelmonotherapy may be at significantly greater risk of further thrombo-occlusive events due to their genetically impaired ability to generate the active form of this drug. The risk ratio in stroke patients appears higher than seen in other cardiovascular indications where additional antiplatelet agents may be used in conjunction with clopidogrel.
