N2  - Introduction: Coronary angioplasty is a procedure that opens narrowed or blocked coronary arteries, following which a stent may be placed to maintain lumen patency. After undergoing the procedure, patients are prescribed a combination of 2 anti-platelet medications, commonly aspirin and clopidogrel (Plavix), to prevent thrombus formation. Clopidogrel is converted to its active form by hepatic enzyme CYP2C19, which has several phenotypic variants. Up to 30% of individuals harbor loss-of-function CYP2C19 alleles. Patients on Clopidogrel who possess these variants have a higher risk of stent thrombosis, heart attack, stroke, and cardiovascular death. The FDA recommends CYP2C19 genotype testing and advises physicians to consider alternative treatment such as prasugrel (Effient) or ticagrelor (Brilinta) in patients with CYP2C19 ∗2 and ∗3 loss-of function variants. The aim of this study is to implement CYP2C19 genotype testing to guide therapy decision-making for patients undergoing percutaneous coronary intervention (PCI) and stent placement. Methods: Spartan RX CYP2C19 genotyping assay was validated in the Molecular Diagnostic Laboratory at UAB Hospital. Since January 2015, CYP2C19 testing has been incorporated into the clinical work flow of patients undergoing PCI and stent placement. Test orders, sample collection, processing and results were integrated within our Cerner electronic health record. Genotypes were resulted as wild type, heterozygous, or homozygous on ∗2, ∗7 and ∗17 alleles (wild type was reported as ∗1). When ∗2 or ∗3 alleles were detected, ordering physicians were notified and provided decision support and alternative treatment recommendations. Results: Genotype testing was ordered and performed on 81 patients. CYP2C19 genotype distribution in these patients was ∗1/∗1 (n=32), ∗1/∗17 (n=23), ∗1/∗2 (n=17), ∗2/∗2 (n=1), ∗2/∗17 (n=4) and ∗17/17 (n=4). In 2 patients (∗1/∗2 and ∗17/∗17) testing was performed twice due to initial inconclusive results. Overall, 27% (n=22) of the patients had heterozygous or homozygous loss of function alleles. Within 1 hour of submitting patient samples, ordering physicians were notified of any clinically significant results and provided decision support for personalized antiplatelet therapy. Conclusions: Spartan RX CYP2C19 Test provides accurate and rapid results to guide clinical decision-making at point-of-care. The 1 hour work flow enables repeat testing (if needed) without delaying treatment decisions or patient discharge. Prospective follow-up and documentation of the clinical course is ongoing. These results will evaluate outcomes and effectiveness of CYP2C19 testing.
