N2  - Objective: To investigate the relationship of CYP2C19 variants and the platelet reactivity pharmacologic response in acute stroke patients treated with clopidogrel. Methods: Patients with acute ischemic stroke treated with a combination of clopidogrel (75 mg) and Aspirin (100 mg) at our hospital. Genotypes and phenotypes of CYP2C19 were determined. Platelet functions were assessed by adenosine diphosphate-induced (ADP) and acetylsalicylic acid (AA)-induced platelet aggregation using thromboelastogram (TEG) after seven days treatment. Results: In total of 130 patients, CYP2C19 phenotypes were classified into 3 groups: extensive metabolizers (EMs; n=57, 43.8%), intermediate metabolizers (IMs; n=60, 46.2%), and poor metabolizers (PMs; n=13, 10%). The percent inhibition of ADP was <20% in patients (29%). 61% patients had the ADP inhibitions in 20-50%. 40% patients had the ADP inhibitions higher than 50%. In the EMs group, the rate percent of ADP inhibition <20% and >50% were significantly different with 8.8% (n=5) and 42.1% (n=24), respectively. Conclusions: Variants of the CYP2C19 significantly associated with clopidogrel response and may guide to personalized antiplatelet therapy in acute ischemic stroke patients.
