N2  - BACKGROUND: Little is known about how clinicians use platelet function testing (PFT) to guide choice of P2Y12 receptor inhibitor therapy in routine community practice. METHODS: We studied 1,045 percutaneous coronary intervention (PCI) treated myocardial infarction (MI) patients with PFT performed while on clopidogrel treatment during index hospitalization using VerifyNow® at 64 United States hospitals from 04/2010-10/2012 in the TRANSLATE-ACS study. Frequency of switch to a higher potency P2Y12 receptor inhibitor (prasugrel and ticagrelor), and associated rates of major adverse cardiovascular event [MACE: death, MI, stroke, or unplanned revascularization] at 1 year were determined among those with high (>208 PRU) and therapeutic platelet reactivity (TPR: ≤208 PRU). Logistic regression modeling was used to determine an estimated rule for which it is optimal to switch in terms of minimizing predicted probability of MACE. RESULTS: The median (25th, 75th percentile) time to PFT was 3.0 (1.8, 6.4) hours post last clopidogrel dose. High platelet reactivity (HPR) was present in 381 (36.5%) patients. Switch to a higher potency P2Y12 receptor inhibitor occurred in 170 (16.3%) patients (prasugrel 151 patients, ticagrelor 19 patients); switch occurred in 127 (33.3%) patients with HPR and 43 (6.5%) patients with TPR. Patients switched to a higher potency P2Y12 receptor inhibitor had greater frequency of multivessel disease (48.2% vs. 58.3%, p=0.03) and higher on clopidogrel platelet reactivity (median, 155 vs. 255 PRU, p<0.0001). Compared with continuation on clopidogrel, switch to a higher potency P2Y12 receptor inhibitor was associated with a lower MACE rate at 1-year among patients with HPR (20.5% vs. 10.2%, p=0.01), but not TPR (14.3 vs. 14.0%, p=0.95) (Figure). Logistic regression modeling demonstrated that MACE rate would be optimized by switching clopidogrel to a more potent P2Y12 receptor inhibitor in all patients with platelet reactivity >129 PRU. CONCLUSION: Among MI patients treated with PCI, intensification of antiplatelet therapy among those with high platelet reactivity on-clopidogrel was associated with lower risk of ischemic events at 1-year. Ischemic outcomes may be improved by intensifying antiplatelet therapy (eg. prasugrel) in patients with lower on-clopidogrel platelet reactivity thresholds. (Figure Presented).
