N2  - Aims: The aim of this study was to evaluate an additive effect of endothelial dysfunction on clinical outcome following coronary stent implantation in patients with a cytochrome P 450 (CYP) 2C19 variant. Methods and results: We enrolled 248 patients following elective percutaneous coronary intervention (PCI). All patients underwent cardiac catheterisation and PCI during hospitalisation, and they received dual antiplatelet therapy with maintenance doses of 100 mg/day of aspirin and 75 mg/day of clopidogrel after a loading dose of 300 mg of clopidogrel. CYP2C19 genotype was examined, and the subjects were divided into two groups; carriers: intermediate metabolisers (IM; ∗1/∗2, ∗1/∗3) and poor metabolisers (PM; ∗2/∗2, ∗2/∗3, ∗3/∗3) carrying at least one CYP2C19 loss-of-function (LOF) allele, and non-carriers: extensive metabolisers (EM; CYP2C19∗1/∗1) not carrying a CYP2C19 LOF allele. Peripheral endothelial dysfunction was examined using reactive hyperaemia-peripheral arterial tonometry index (RHI), and we divided patients into low and high RHI. Thus, subjects were divided into four groups: carriers with low RHI (n=77), carriers with high RHI (n=89), non-carriers with low RHI (n=45) and non-carriers with high RHI (n=37). The endpoint was a composite of cardiovascular death, nonfatal myocardial infarction, ischaemic stroke, hospitalisation due to unstable angina pectoris and coronary revascularisation, and clinical events were followed up. Clinical characteristics were similar among four groups, and there was no significant difference in RHI between carrier and non-carrier (0.61±0.23 vs. 0.55±0.23; P=0.071). The mean follow-up period was 917 days and the median follow-up period was 857 days. We followed patients up to 1,726 days in this study. A total of 23 patients had a cardiovascular event. Details of the cardiovascular events are as follows: cardiovascular death (n=1), nonfatal myocardial infarction (n=0), stroke (n=3), unstable angina (n=3) and coronary revascularisation (n=16). Kaplan-Meier analysis demonstrated a significantly higher probability of cardiovascular events in carriers with low RHI (log-rank test: P=0.015). Multivariate Cox proportional hazards analysis identified being a carrier with low RHI as an independent and significant predictor of future cardiovascular events (hazard ratio: 3.73; 95% confidence interval: 1.62-8.63; P=0.002). Conclusions: CYP2C19 LOF allele carriers with endothelial dysfunction were significantly correlated with cardiovascular events. The additional evaluation of endothelial function along with CYP2C19 polymorphism might improve risk stratification after coronary stent implantation.
