N2  - Introduction Clopidogrel, an inhibitor of platelet aggregation, is commonly prescribed to prevent thrombotic events. Clopidogrel increases the risk of gastrointestinal bleeding, so proton pump inhibitors (PPIs) are commonly prescribed to avoid clopidogrel-related gastrointestinal bleeding. As clopidogrel requires bio activation by the cytochrome P450 enzyme, CYP2C19, PPIs may reduce the antithrombotic efficacy of clopidogrel by inhibiting CYP2C19. Several studies in Caucasian populations receiving both omeprazole and clopidogrel showed no significant increases in death and myocardial infarction with this clopidogrel-omeprazole drug-drug interaction. We and others have previously shown that, compared with Caucasian persons, persons of Asian descent have a greater prevalence of CYP2C19 loss of function polymorphisms. Methods Consecutive patients prescribed clopidogrel for any indication from 2009- 2012 at tertiary medical centre were followed up till August 2014 (range of follow-up 31-67 months). The primary outcome was all-cause mortality, and secondary outcomes were acute myocardial infarction and cerebrovascular accidents. Results Of 12440 patients prescribed clopidogrel, 62% (n=7714) were also on PPI, and 38% (n=4726) were not on PPI. Of the patients prescribed clopidogrel without PPI, 62.6% (n=2944) were Chinese, 13.5% (n=635) were Malay, 10.7% (n=623) were Indian, and 13.2% (623) were non-Asian. The percentages of patients prescribed clopidogrel with PPI were 63.8% (n= 4910), 13.9% (n=1067), 12.4% (n=956) and 10% (n=767) respectively. Mortality after prescription occurred in 14.3% (n=1101) of patients prescribed clopidogrel with PPI, as compared to 6.3% (n=300) of patients prescribed clopidogrel without PPI. In multivariate regression analysis using inverse probability weighted estimates, the use of clopidogrel with PPI was associated with an increase in all-cause mortality compared to the use of clopidogrel without PPI (adjusted odds ratio [AOR] 1.52, 95% confidence interval [CI] 1.26-1.85). Patients taking clopidogrel with PPI had a higher risk of myocardial infarctions (16% vs 3.8%; AOR, 3.00 [95% CI, 2.48-3.63]), but not of cerebrovascular accidents (5.0% vs 2.0%; AOR, 1.15 [95% CI, 0.87-1.51]). Use of clopidogrel with PPI was associated with a greater risk of mortality and myocardial infarctions among this study population. Conclusion Combination use of clopidogrel and PPI is associated with an increased risk of all cause mortality and myocardial infarction among in a predominantly Asian population. Further research is needed to determine if differential prevalence of CYP2C19 polymorphisms are responsible for these findings.
