N2  - Aims: In patients who underwent PCI with second-generation DES, using quantitative coronary angiographic analysis (QCA) and assessed clinical outcomes we measured platelet reactivity after clopidogrel loading and late lumen loss. Methods and results: Platelet reactive units (PRU) were measured by Verify Now P2Y12 assays in 396 patients who underwent PCI with everolimusor biolimuseluting stents between August 2011 and June 2013. All patients received 300 mg clopidogrel loading immediately after PCI, 369 patients were followed by a maintenance dose of clopidogrel (7550 mg/d) and aspirin (100 mg/d). Two hundred and ninet yeight (298) patients had undergone coronary angiography (CAG) at 1012 months follow-up. Blood samples were obtained 12 to 24 hours after PCI (initial phase) and two to four weeks later (chronic phase). High platelet reactivity (HPR) on clopidogrel was defined as 240 and more in PRU. We assessed major adverse cardiovascular events (MACE) including the composite of death, non-fatal MI, stent thrombosis, stroke, target vessel revascularisation (TVR), and bleeding events. QCA was evaluated by baseline and follow-up CAG. The value of PRU and rate of HPR in the chronic phase were significantly lower than in the initial phase (249±81 vs. 214±83; p<0.0001, 58% vs. 42%; p<0.0001, respectively). In the chronic phase, the HPR group had more females, current smokers and elderly patients than the non-HPR group. There were no significant differences in clinical outcomes between HPR and non-HPR (MACE: 7.7% vs. 7.0%; p=0.8 and bleeding events: 5.8% vs. 3.3%; p=0.3, respectively). According to QCA, late lumen loss was significantly greater in the HPR group as compared with the non-HPR group (0.29±0.40 mm vs. 0.17±0.37 mm; p=0.01, respectively). Multiple logistic regression analysis indicated that HPR was the only independent predictor for late lumen loss of 0.3 mm and over (p=0.008). Conclusions: After PCI with second-generation DES, there was no significant impact of platelet reactivity after clopidogrel loading on clinical outcomes. However, adequate inhibition of platelet reactivity reduced late lumen loss at follow-up.
