N2  - Introduction: Hemodynamic stress, systemic inflammation, and platelet reactivity are key pathways mediating outcomes after acute coronary syndrome (ACS), but it remains uncertain how circulating biomarkers that reflect these pathways are associated and interconnected with ischemic outcomes in ACS patients who are managed without revascularization. Methods: We measured N-terminal pro B-type natriuretic peptide (NTproBNP; marker of hemodynamic stress), high-sensitivity C-reactive protein (hs-CRP; marker of systemic inflammation), and P2Y12 reaction units (PRU; marker of platelet reactivity measured by the VerifyNow device) at baseline and at 6 months in 1219 patients with non-ST-elevation ACS managed without revascularization and randomized to prasugrel vs clopidogrel in the TRILOGY-ACS trial. We tested the independent association of NTproBNP, hs-CRP, and PRU values with ischemic outcomes using Cox regression to adjust for baseline characteristics, including prior heart failure, Killip class, and biomarker results. Results: A total of 115 cardiovascular (CV) death, myocardial infarction (MI), or stroke events occurred during 24 months of follow-up. The unadjusted cumulative incidence of the composite primary endpoint of CV death, MI and stroke was 11.3% (CV death, 4.5%; MI, 7.5%; and stroke, 1.3%). In multivariable analysis, higher baseline and 6-month NTproBNP values were significantly associated with CV death while higher 6-month, but not baseline, hs-CRP measurements were significantly associated with MI (Table). PRU measurements were not associated with the events analyzed. Conclusions: Longitudinal measurements of circulating biomarkers reflecting hemodynamic stress and systemic inflammation, but not platelet reactivity, delineate independent but complementary prognostic information in patients with non-ST-elevation ACS who are managed without revascularization and treated with either prasugrel or clopidogrel.
