N2  - Introduction: The antiplatelet agent Clopidogrel (Plavix) undergoes a two-step hepatic P450 CYP2C19 metabolism and is reportedly less efficacious in patients who harbor ∗2 and ∗3 loss-of function CYP2C19 alleles. Such patients remain at increased risk for heart attack, stroke, and cardiovascular death despite therapy. The ∗17 gain-offunction variant has an unknown clinical significance. With the recent availability of point-of-care testing for CYP2C19, treatment decisions can be made when patients present for percutaneous coronary intervention and stent placement. We decided to validate Nanosphere's Verigene CYP2C19 test, the first FDA approved point-of-care testing which measures the ∗2, ∗3, and ∗17 variants. Methods: From October 2013 to March 2014 peripheral blood was collected in EDTA from 96 samples. The CYP2C19 genotype was measured using Verigene CYP2C19 test according to manufacturer's procedure. In addition, 3 control samples were run by hybridization mode. The genotype was resulted as wild type, heterozygous, or mutant on ∗2, ∗3 and ∗17 alleles. The no call rate was monitored, and results were compared to those acquired by other methodologies (e.g., 23andMe). Results: A total of 99 tests were performed with a no call rate of 33% (n=33). Ten samples required repeating, including 8 that remained as no calls despite retesting. Thirteen of 14 patients with previously known genotypes had their alleles confirmed, but one of 3 patients with ∗1/∗17 had two consecutive no calls. The remaining patients who were ∗1/∗2 (n=3), ∗1/∗1 (n=2), and ∗17/∗17 (n=1) had results consistent with their previously known genotypes. Control samples run in hybridization mode also had a 33% no call rate. The manufacturer provided us with 7 whole blood samples; the 27 tests using those samples resulted in 30% no calls (n=8). The product was temporarily withdrawn from the market. Conclusions: The CYP2C19 genotype call was accurate when Verigene provides a genotype result; however, we faced an unexpectedly high no call rate, the reasons of which are unclear. Our validation results differ significantly from the previously published and package insert no call rate of 7% to 10%. Due to the time-sensitive nature of point-of-care testing, we feel that Verigene's CYP2C19 test is not a feasible option at this time. Alternative point-of-care testing such as with Spartan RX CYP2C19 System may be considered.
