N2  - Genetic variants are thought to contribute to the wide intraindividual variability in antiplatelet and anticoagulant drug response. Pharmacogenetics is the study of how genetic variants influence drug response and how the adoption of a more personalized approach in antiplatelet and anticoagulant therapy may help to minimize harmful drug effects and optimize care for individual patients. However, due to sometimes conflicting evidence, the uptake of pharmacogenetics in the clinical setting has been slow. In this presentation, we review the genetic mechanisms contributing to the variability in response to four commonly used and emerging antiplatelet and anticoagulant drug therapies for stroke prevention, namely aspirin, clopidogrel, dabigatran and warfarin.We will focus on common genetic variants that influence the absorption,metabolism and/or action of these agents, including COX2, CYP2C19, CYP3A4, CYP3A5, CYP2C9, ABCB1, P2RY12, CYP2C9, VKORC1 and CES1. Lecture fees received from Boehringer Ingelheim. Grant funding support from Sanofi.
