N2  - Aim: To investigate the methylation status of ABCB1 gene promoter in whole blood of patients with ischemic stroke in relation to ABCB1 mRNA expressions and the antiplatelet effects of clopidogrel. Methods: This study was a prospective cohort analysis of eligible stroke patients (n = 183, aged 18∼75 years) who received clopidogrel (75 mg/day) for at least 5 days before discharge. A final subcohort of 87 patients with CYP2C19∗1/∗1 genotype were enrolled in the study population. The whole blood samples were obtained to evaluate the promoter methylation status and mRNA expression of ABCB1. Pyrosequencing was carried out to investigate ABCB1 gene methylation status, Total RNA was extracted from the whole blood and ABCB1 mRNA expression was evaluated by Quantitative PCR (qPCR). The antiplatelet effect of clopidogrel was evaluated by detection of maximum platelet aggregation (MPA). Patients were grouped in quartiles of MPA values (Q1, Q2, Q3 and Q4, MPAQ1 <14.1%, MPAQ4 >35.4%). Results: The methylation status of the ABCB1 promoter was 1.8 times in the Q1 MPA group (10.1%±2.4%) than in the Q4 MPA group (5.5%±2.1%) (P < 0.001). ABCB1 methylation correlated inversely with MPA (R = -0.764, P < 0.001) and mRNA expression (R = -0.839, P < 0.001), respectively. Results of a multivariate linear regression model demonstrated that ABCB1 methylation was independently associated with MPA (βcoefficient = -4.71, P < 0.001). ABCB1 expression was 0.62 times in the Q1 MPA group (5.3%±1.4%) than in the Q4 MPA (8.5%±2.5%), and the expression of ABCB1 correlated positively with ADP-induced MPA (R = 0.791, P < 0.001). Conclusions: Hypomethylation of ABCB1 promoter is associated with the decreased response to clopidogrel in ischemic stroke patients via increased ABCB1 mRNA expression.
