N2  - OBJECTIVE: The present data examines platelet responsiveness to ASA in 33 secondary stroke prophylaxis patients treated with ASA alone versus the same dose of ASA in combination with clopidogrel.BACKGROUND: Aspirin (ASA) and clopidogrel, which are commonly used for secondary stroke prophylaxis, inhibit platelet aggregation by blocking the COX 1 enzyme and ADP receptors, respectively.DESIGN/METHODS: Platelet response was assessed using whole blood aggregation. ASA effect was measured in ohms of resistance to collagen 1 μg/mL and arachidonic acid (AA) 0.5mM. Platelet non-responsiveness to ASA was defined as >10 ohms of resistance to collagen 1 μg/ml and/or an ohms ratio of collagen 1 μg/ml to collagen 5 μg/ml >0.5 and/or > 6 ohms of resistance to AA. Patients were excluded if they were concomitantly taking NSAIDs with ASA or were noncompliant as determined by lack of salicylates in their urine the day of testing.RESULTS: The mean aggregation to collagen 1 μg/mL decreased from 10.24±5.82 ohms on ASA alone to 4.24±2.42 ohms on ASA and clopidogrel (paired t-test p<0.001). The mean aggregation to AA decreased from 4.61±6.56 ohms on ASA alone to 0.97±3.78 ohms on dual therapy (unpaired t-test p<0.01). Eleven of 33 patients (33%) were non-responsive to aspirin monotherapy while only 1 of the 33 patients (3%) remained non-responsive to ASA on combination therapy irrespective of platelet responsiveness to clopidogrel (McNemar's test p<0.001). Significantly fewer patients experienced recurrent stroke, transient ischemic attack (TIA) or TIA-like symptoms, on combination (6%) than on ASA alone (27%) (McNemar's test p<0.001).CONCLUSIONS: Despite differences in mechanism of action, these data demonstrate a synergism between clopidogrel and the antiplatelet effects of ASA. Therefore, the addition of clopidogrel to an aspirin regimen can overcome aspirin resistance and consequently may be warranted in certain high risk patient populations, such as those who are non-responsive to both agents individually.
