N2  - Background: Clopidogrel(CLP) labeling includes a warning about reduced effectiveness in CYP2C19 poor metabolizers, in whom ticagrelor(TCG) may be more effective. The cost-effectiveness of combined genotype and platelet reactivity assay (PRA) driven approach to selecting either TCG versus CLP in patients with acute coronary syndrome (ACS) is unclear. Methods: Six strategies - conservative TCG (TCG only in CYP2C19∗2 homozygotes), liberal TCG (TCG for CYP2C19∗2 homo and heterozygotes), genotype-PRA combination (TCG only in patients with CYP2C19 polymorphisms and CLP nonresponse by PRA), PRA driven selection, universal TCG and universal CLP - were compared from the U.S. health system perspective. A decision model described the chances of complications including myocardial infarction (MI), ischemic stroke (IS), vascular death, and bleeding events during one year of treatment. Cost and utility inputs were identified from literature review and the Healthcare Cost and Utilization Project (HCUP) database. The cost of genotype and PRA was obtained by expert opinion. All costs were adjusted to 2012 US dollars. Lifetime costs and quality adjusted life years (QALYs) were projected with a Markov model. One-way sensitivity analyses and Monte Carlo simulation were performed. Results: In the base case analysis, conservative TCG ($41,093, 7.99 QALYs) and genotype-PRA combination ($41,501, 8.06 QALYs) were dominated (extended) by PRA-driven selection ($41,363, 8.05 QALYs) and liberal TCG ($41,605, 8.07 QALYs), respectively. The incremental costeffectiveness ratio (ICER) for the universal TCG use was $85,429/QALY compared to liberal TCG, and ICER for this genotype strategy over other alternatives did not exceed $50,000/QALY. Conclusions were generally robust, although universal TCG did not achieve 80% likelihood of being costeffective up to a willingness to pay threshold of $1,000,000/QALY. Conclusions: At current costs and test characteristics, genotype-guided TCG was preferred over PRA-driven drug selection. However, universal TCG may be a preferred option if one is willing to accept the considerable uncertainty in its cost-effectiveness compared to genotype guided approach.
