N2  - Background: Patients requiring CABG surgery are often exposed to the thienopyridine drug clopidogrel. The ACC/AHA recommends holding clopidogrel for 5 days before CABG to reduce the risk of bleeding. There is some evidence, however, that patients may be able to undergo surgery as soon as 3 days after the last dose of clopidogrel. The Society of Thoracic Surgeons (STS) recommends using platelet reactivity testing to aid in the timing of surgery instead of arbitrarily waiting a pre-specified period of time. The aim of this study was to determine if the use of a point of care platelet assay could decrease the time it takes for patients on clopidogrel to reach an acceptable platelet inhibition level prior to CABG. Methods: This was a single center, retrospective study determining the time it took for patients who underwent CABG with a known last dose of clopidogrel to reach a platelet reactivity testing level suitable for reducing the risk of perioperative bleeding. The VerifyNow P2Y12 PRU test was used. A P2Y12 Reaction Unit (PRU) value ≥230 was selected based on studies that have found this value to be associated with less bleeding. Exclusion criteria included emergent CABG with PRU<230 on last testing, concurrent antiplatelet medications other than aspirin, creatinine≥2.5, platelet count<100,000/μl, or delayed platelet reactivity testing. Results: A total of 47 patients were identified. The median time from last dose of clopidogrel to PRU ≥230 was 2 days (n=47, IQR 1-4 days, range 8 days). A median of 2 tests were performed. A history of MI, CAD, diabetes, or stroke had no effect on the time it took for patients to achieve a PRU≥230. Male sex, concurrent beta-blockers, calcium channel blockers, statins, proton pump inhibitors, ACE inhibitors/ARBs, or use of furosemide similarly had no effect on results. Conclusion: In this study, achieving a PRU ≥230 after a last dose of clopidogrel took from 0 to 8 days, with a median of 2 days, significantly less than the recommended 5 days of waiting. With an approximate cost of $60 per test and a median of 2 tests per patient, the use of this assay would be expected to be extremely cost effective in reducing wait times to surgery and ensuring a reduced risk of perioperative bleeding.
