N2  - Purpose: To assess the clinical implications and optimal cutoff value of high platelet reactivity (HPR) in patients receiving clopidogrel for peripheral endovascular procedures (PEP). Materials and Methods: This prospective trial included patients receiving clopidogrel 75 mg daily, prior and after infrainguinal angioplasty or stenting (NCT01744613). Platelet inhibition was assessed with the VerifyNow P2Y12 point-ofcare test. Primary endpoints were 1-year clinical event rate (composite endpoint of death, major stroke, major amputation, target vessel revascularization, bypass) according to the P2Y12 reaction Units (PRU)-based quartile distribution, the estimation of the optimal PRU cut-off value to predict clinical outcome and the identification of independent predictors influencing event-free survival. Results: In total, 100 consecutive patients were enrolled. The 1-year cumulative event rate was 4% in the first, 12%, in the second, 52% in the third and 84% in the fourth quartile. Pairwise comparisons demonstrated a significant difference in the composite endpoint between successive quartiles (p<0.05 except for the first vs. second quartile). According to ROC analysis the optimal cut-off value for the composite endpoint was PRUZ234 (area under the curve 0.883; 95%CI: 0.811- 0.954; p<0.0001, sensitivity 92.1%, specificity 84.2%). Cox multivariable regression analysis identified HPR (PRUZ234) as the only independent predictor for increased number of adverse events (HR: 16.9; 95%CI: 5-55, p<0.0001). Conclusion: High on treatment platelet reactivity is associated with markedly increased adverse clinical events in patients undergoing PEP. Platelet function testing might be useful in individualizing antiplatelet therapy as to attain superior clinical results.
