N2  - Background: Epidemiologic studies have suggested the pharmacologic effect varies of clopidogrel based on CYP3A4 genotype; however, uncertainty still remained regarding to the clinical risk imparted by specific genotypes. Objective: To determine whether an association exists between CYP3A4 gene polymorphism and clopidogrel resistance in observational studies with meta-analysis. Design: We searched PubMed, Cochrane database of systematic Reviews and Embase for studies conducted from the database established to June 2013. Study-specific risk estimates were combined by using a fixed-effects model. Results: A total of 8 studies were included in the meta-analysis, consisting of 7 case-control studies and 1 prospective cohort studies. Among 15497 patients, 1229 (7.93%) patients experienced the composite end point of cardiovascular death, myocardial infarction, or stroke. Overall, 10713 (69.10%) were CYP3A4 gene polymorphism and 4784 (30.90%) were non-carriers. A significantly increased risk of the composite end point was evident in carriers reduced-function CYP3A4 alleles, as compared with non-carriers (OR, 1.31; 95% CI ,1.12-1.55; Z = 3.30, P = 0.0010; Q statistic test for heterogeneity: x 2 = 12.61, P = 0.08, I2 = 44%). Conclusions: Carriage of reduced-function CYP3A4 allele appears to be associated with a significantly increased risk of major adverse cardiovascular events in patients treated with clopidogrel for percutaneous coronary intervention.
