N2  - Objective: To investigate the effect of CYP2C19 gene polymorphism on the efficacy of standard-dose clopidogrel plus aspirin in patients with transient ischemic attack (TIA).
Methods: The genetic polymorphism of CYP2C19 was identified, then the efficacy of clopidogrel in different CYP2C19*2 genotype groups was compared.
Results: A total of 573 TIA patients participated in the clinical study after genetic testing, among whom 56 carrying CYP2C19*2 homozygotes, 244 carrying CYP2C19*2 allele heterozygotes. The patients were assigned to wild type group (n=273) and mutant group (n=300). The statistical analysis showed that the two groups had similar demographic characteristics at baseline (P>0.05). Clopidogrel had weaker protective effect in patients with CYP2C19*2 allele (mutant group) than in patients with wild-type taking the same dose of clopidogrel (OR=6.12, 95% confidence interval [4.37, 8.60], P=0.001), while with less bleeding events (OR=10.19, 95% confidence interval [1.28, 80.99], P=0.017).
Conclusion: CYP2C19*2 gene mutation may lead to decreased platelet reactivity and significantly lower risk of bleeding in patients taking clopidogrel. It is suggested that CYP2C19*2 genetic testing be taken, and the dose of clopidogrel be adjusted according to the count of platelets to reduce bleeding events.
