N2  - OBJECTIVES: To investigate association of CYP2C19 polymorphisms and proton-pump inhibitors (PPI) therapy with efficacy of clopidogrel in patients with coronary atherosclerotic heart disease. METHODS: A total of 189 Chinese Han patients who underwent percutaneous coronary intervention and treated with clopidogrel were enrolled. Polymorphisms of CYP2C19∗2, ∗3 were analyzed using PCR-restriction fragment length polymorphism (PCR-RFLP). Genotyping of CYP2C19 was classified as extensive (∗1/∗1), intermediate (∗1/∗2, ∗1/∗3), and poor (∗2/∗2, ∗2/∗3, ∗3/∗3) metabolize genotype. We examined the association between these genetic variants and PPI combined therapy with cardiovascular outcomes. RESULTS: The allele frequency of CYP2C19∗2 and ∗3 was 33.86% and 5.82%. 13.76% had poor metabolize genotype (∗2/∗2, ∗2/∗3, ∗3/∗3). Of the 189 patients, 11 experienced acute coronary syndrome, 1 suffered stroke, 4 patients had stent thrombosis and 1 died. The overall incidence of cardiovascular events was 8.99%. Poor metabolize genotype carriers were not associated with high cardiovascular events (χ2 = 0.23, p = 0.63). And PPI combined therapy did not increase the incidence of cardiovascular events (χ2 = 0.0002, p = 0.98). CONCLUSIONS: The overall frequencies of CYP2C19 poor metabolize genotype in this Chinese Han population were higher than those in Caucasians, but frequency of cardiovascular events in Chinese Han population did not increase. Factors other than CYP2C19 polymorphisms and PPI therapy should be considered for clopidogrel resistance.
