N2  - Over 2000 articles on pharmacogenetics were published in 2012, all aiming at improving individual therapy. Within the cardiovascular field, several markers have been put forward. CYP2C9/VKORC1 variant alleles have been indicated as potential predictors of warfarin therapy since 2008. Together with physical parameters, these markers explained 50% of variation in warfarin dose requirement. Acomparative trial indicated that genotyping would result in 28% less hospitalizations due to bleeding or thromboembolism whereas cost-effectiveness analyses were positive in case genetic tests were available b$200 and available within 24 h. Information on CYP2C9/VKORC1 has been taken up in the drug label of warfarin, together with specific dose recommendations. In 2009, the effectivity of clopidogrel, an antiplatelet drug, was shown to be dependent on CYP2C19 and ABCB1 variant alleles, in which carriership of deficient alleles was correlated with a higher risk on myocardial infarction, stroke or death. These findings have both been confirmed and rejectedin recent meta-analyses, raising confusion on the clinical usefulness. For simvastatin, SLCO1B1 genetic polymorphisms were in 2008 correlated with risk on myopathy, with an odds ratio of 16 for the homozygous variant (NEJM 2008, SEARCH group). This has resulted in dose recommendations in 2012 (CPT 2012, CPIC). Recently, the CYP3A4∗22 allele was correlated with a higher effect of statins in lowering serum cholesterol. With respect to hypertension, a large study in 2011 (Brugts et al.) identified four genetic polymorphisms that were associated with hypertension, and genetic polymorphisms that significantly modified blood pressure reduction by ACE-inhibitor therapy. So many pharmacogenetic markers have been found, but are they used in routine diagnostics?.
