N2  - Anti-platelet agents (APA) are widely used in the secondary prevention of ischaemic stroke but about 30% of patients derive suboptimal platelet inhibition from APA. An underlying cause for suboptimal platelet inhibition is varying response to clopidogrel, which is linked to polymorphisms in the CYP2C19 gene responsible for the metabolism and activation of clopidogrel. CYP2C19 polymorphism influences clinical outcomes in patients with coronary artery disease, particularly among those treated with percutaneous transluminal coronary artery stenting. Randomized controlled trials have shown that high doses of clopidogrel can overcome suboptimal platelet response in carriers of the CYP2C19*2 allele. The United States Food and Drug Administration has issued a boxed warning advising clinicians to consider genotyping patients prior to prescribing clopidogrel. There are ongoing studies investigating the clinical utility of genotyping to inform management decisions in stroke prevention. © 2012 Elsevier Ltd. All rights reserved.
