N2  - Aims: Although the cytochrome P450 (CYP) 2C19∗17 variant allele has been associated with increased platelet response to clopidogrel and risk of bleeding in Westerners, pronounced interethnic variability is seen in the prevalence and genotype pattern of this allele and limited data are available on long-term clinical outcomes during clopidogrel exposure in Asians. Methods and results: We consecutively enrolled 2,188 patients undergoing PCI followed by dual antiplatelet therapy. The prevalence of CYP2C19∗17 variant allele (heterozygote [∗wt/∗17]: 2.4% [n=53], homozygote [∗17/∗17]: 0% [n=0]) was very low compared with wild-type homozygotes (∗wt/∗wt: 97.6% [n=2,135]). The median duration of clopidogrel exposure was 1.33 years (interquartile range 0.78-1.99). The 2-year cumulative Kaplan-Meier event rates for combined TIMI major and minor bleeding (∗wt/∗17 vs. ∗wt/∗wt: 2% vs. 2.3%; adjusted hazard ratio [HR], 1.23; 95% confidence interval [CI], 0.16-9.45), stent thrombosis (∗wt/∗17 vs. ∗wt/∗wt: 2% vs. 1.1%; HR, 3.98; 95% CI, 0.49-31.6) or composite of any death, nonfatal myocardial infarction and stroke (∗wt/∗17 vs. ∗wt/∗wt: 5.4% vs. 7.1%; HR, 1.37; 95% CI, 0.32-5.73) did not differ according to CYP2C19∗17 carriage. On multivariate logistic analysis, CYP2C19∗17 carriage was not an independent predictor for bleeding (odds ratio, 1.57; 95% CI, 0.21-12.2). Conclusions: The CYP2C19∗17 polymorphism may not be associated with adverse clinical outcomes in Asian patients taking clopidogrel after PCI. Its limited impact is explained partly by lower prevalence, the rarity of homozygotes and relatively low rate of adverse clinical events.
