N2  - Background: Antiplatelet therapy is associated with decreased ischaemic events post neurointerventional procedures. Antiplatelet resistance negates the protective effects of antiplatelets leading to higher incidence of ischaemic events. A possible link between antiplatelet hyperresponsiveness and increased haemorrhagic complications has been inadequately investigated. Aim: To examine the correlation between antiplatelet hyperresponsiveness and risk of haemorrhagic complications in neurointervention. Methods: Patients who were pretreated with antiplatelet medications prior to neurointerventional procedures were prospectively recruited. The following data were collected: demographics, vascular risk factors, antiplatelet and anticoagulation treatment, antiplatelet responsiveness, coagulation profile and haemorrhagic complications. P2Y12 receptor mediated platelet inhibition was tested using the VerifyNow assay device. The primary endpoints were post-procedural major and minor haemorrhagic complications. Percentage platelet inhibition was evaluated as a diagnostic tool for bleeding events Receiver Operator Characteristics analysis. Association between haemorrhage and percentage platelet inhibition was investigated using logistic regression modelling. Results: Forty-seven patients were enrolled. The mean age was 56 ± 12 and 28% were male. Ten patients (21.3%) developed minor and three major haemorrhagic complications. Clopidogrel response was higher in patients with a major bleeding complication compared to those with minor or no bleeding (median 94% vs. 24% platelet inhibition, P = 0.0084). of the seven patients (14.9%) defined as hyperresponsive with ≥72% platelet inhibition, 42.8% had a major bleeding complication. Conclusion: Hyperresponse to clopidogrel is associated with increased risk of haemorrhagic complications. Larger studies are urgently needed to validate a clinically useful threshold to define hyperresponsiveness, and to examine the clinical effects of antiplatelet dosage adjustment.
