N2  - Platelet resistance, or resistance to antiplatelet medication, can be defined as failure of the antiplatelet agent to produce the desired effect of inhibiting platelet aggregation. Platelet inhibitors or antiplatelet agents are widely prescribed to prevent and treat acute vascular events such as myocardial infarction and transient ischaemic attacks. In interventional practice, antiplatelet therapy is essential for both premedication and maintenance treatment of patients receiving endovascular therapies such as coronary or cerebrovascular stents. However, some patients do not respond, or respond suboptimally, to antiplatelet therapy, with consequent increased risk of morbidity and mortality if this is not recognised. Understanding the role of platelets in haemostasis and the mechanism of action of antiplatelet agents helps us to understand why these drugs sometimes fail. The increasing availability of platelet function testing has led to improved patient management, but the available platelet function testing devices differ in how and what they measure, and so interpretation of test results may be challenging. There are three classes of platelet inhibitors: aspirin, thienopyridine derivatives, such as clopidogrel (Plavix), and GP IIb/IIIa receptor antagonists such as Abciximab (Reopro). Only the first two classes are of use for long term treatment of patients. Platelets are small short lived cells that circulate in the bloodstream in inactive form; once activated they are integral to primary haemostasis. Active platelets use cyclooxygenases COX1 and COX2 to produce prostaglandin H2, from which Thromboxane A2 is synthesised. Thromboxane A2 stimulates platelet activation and also stimulates the expression of GIIb/IIIa receptors on the surface of the platelet. These receptors are the linking sites for fibrinogen, allowing platelets to aggregate together. Binding of fibrinogen to GII/IIIa receptors to link platelets together requires ADP, which has to bind to a P2Y12 receptor to facilitate fibrinogen attachment. Aspirin irreversibly binds to COX1, rendering it inactive, and so impairs synthesis of Thromboxane A2. This results in reduced activation of additional platelets and prevention of expression of GII/IIIa receptors on the platelet surface. Clopidogrel blocks the P2Y12 receptor, preventing ADP from attaching and therefore preventing aggregation of platelets by fibrinogen. The prevalence of aspirin resistance has been quoted as up to 60%, indicating the variation in definitions, assays and patient populations. Clinical, cellular and genetic factors all contribute to resistance. Clopidogrel resistance has a quoted prevalence of up to 30%, with contributory factors including dose, interaction with other medications and liver function. Platelet function testing adds another layer of complexity, as different types of testing machines, using different reagents, unsurprisingly may give results that differ with each other. There is a move away from 'gold standard' laboratory aggregometers towards 'point of care' testing, leading to earlier treatment modification. Questions remain on whether platelet function testing is relevant in practice, and if so in which situations. Much of the research performed in the cardiac disease population for example may not be directly transferable to the neurovascular population, where the consequences of treatment failure have different implications for patient outcome. Dose, duration of antiplatelet treatment, platelet function testing and strategies for managing platelet resistance in different populations would all benefit from further research.
