N2  - Objective: To evaluate the frequency and effects of variability in clopidogrel CYP2C19 metabolism in cerebrovascular disease patients to determine the value of genetic testing in risk stratification. Background It has been estimated that 3% (range 2-14%) of the population are poor clopidogrel metabolizers, with significant racial variations. The presence of genetic variation lacks clinical significance unless patient outcomes are affected and there exists available alternative therapies. While poor/intermediate clopidogrel metabolizers have been shown to have more adverse cardiovascular events, little data is available on the prevalence of poor/intermediate metabolizers and the frequency of recurrent events in stroke/TIA patients. Design/Methods: Retrospective review of all stroke and TIA patients tested for the clopidogrel CYP2C19 genotype from 4/2010 to 10/2011 was performed. Demographic data was collected including age, race, CYP2C19 status, comorbidities, social risk factors, and presence of recurrent vascular events. Results: 43 stroke or TIA patients were tested for the CYP2C19 gene. 4.7% were poor (n=2), 27.9% intermediate (n=12), 62.8% extensive (n=27), and 4.7% indeterminate (n=2) metabolizers. Only 8/30 Caucasians (27%) were intermediate or poor metabolizers, compared with 6/13 (46%) non-Caucasians. At last known follow-up, of 36 patients treated with clopidogrel, none of the 23 extensive metabolizers had recurrent vascular events or thrombosis, as compared with 27% of intermediate metabolizers (3/11), and 100% of poor and indeterminate metabolizers (1/1 each). Conclusions: These data suggest that comparable proportions of poor and intermediate clopidogrel metabolizers are present in cerebrovascular patients as in cardiovascular studies, with a similarly higher prevalence of reduced metabolism in non-Caucasians. The rate of recurrent events was also higher in poor/intermediate metabolizers. Given the significant proportion of patients who are poor/intermediate metabolizers and the associated increased risk of recurrent events, consideration should be given to routine CYP2C19 testing of clopidogrel treated stroke/TIA patients, given the availability of alternative therapies for secondary stroke prevention.
