N2  - Background: Critical Limb Ischemia (CLI) is a major cause of cardiovascular death and disability. Despite aggressive medical therapy the mortality of patients with CLI approaches 25% and 50% at one and five years respectively. Cardiovascular events (myocardial infarction, sudden cardiac death and stroke) are the main causes of death in patients with CLI. In patients with coronary artery disease, residual high on treatment (aspirin and/or clopidogrel) platelet reactivity (HPR) (often misrepresented as “resistance”) is associated with increased cardiac events. The effectiveness of antiplatelet therapy in CLI patients has not been established. The prevalence of HPR in patients with CLI is unknown. Methods: We tested 14 patients with CLI who underwent endovascular procedures at the University of Southern California Hospital on uninterrupted dual antiplatelet therapy with aspirin and clopidogrel for at least one week. The VerifyNow aspirin and P2Y12 (clopidogrel) assays were used to identify the group of patients with HPR. HPR groups were defined as aspirin reaction units (ARU) ≥550 for aspirin and P2Y12 reaction units (PRU) ≥235 for clopidogrel. Baseline demographic, clinical and procedural characteristics were also recorded. Results: In the overall group 75% of patients were male above 60 years of age. All patients were hypertensive controlled on medications and over 80% had diabetes mellitus with 50% requiring insulin therapy. Coronary artery disease was present in 55% of patients. HPR to clopidogrel was found in 78.5% of patients, HPR to aspirin in 28.5% and HPR to both aspirin and clopidogrel in 14%. Conclusion: HPR to aspirin and/or clopidogrel is highly prevalent in patients with CLI, higher than in any other reported patient population. Further studies are necessary to investigate whether HPR accounts for the excessive cardiovascular mortality and event rate in patients with CLI.
