N2  - Background: Current STEMI guidelines recommend early reperfusion by percutaneous coronary angioplasty (PCI) within 120 min, however there are often delays in transport to PCI centres. Early, prehospital initiation of antiplatelet agents may improve STEMI outcomes, by optimising the antithrombotic milieu by the time of percutaneous coronary intervention (PCI), or 'spontaneous' reperfusion. However, this strategy has not been adequately tested; consequently there is disparity in Australia in initiation of antiplatelet therapy by ambulance personnel. Clopidogrel is often used in this setting, however has a delayed onset of action and variability in platelet inhibition. Ticagrelor, a reversible direct-acting P2Y12-receptor antagonist, offers more rapid, intense and consistent platelet inhibition than clopidogrel (within 30 min of loading dose 41% vs. 8%, respectively), and in PLATOled to better outcomes than clopidogrel with longterm (One year) treatment post-STEMI. The aim of the ATLANTIC study (EudraCT NCT01347580) is to determine whether pre-hospital ticagrelor improves outcomes in STEMI patients undergoing primary PCI. Methods: Patients are being enrolled in a randomised, double-blind controlled trial in 12 countries, including Australia. Initiation of ticagrelor in STEMI patients randomised to early treatment will be by ambulance personnel, and outcomes compared to in-hospital initiation. The primary endpoint is rate of spontaneous reperfusion prior to PCI. The key secondary endpoint is the composite of death, MI, stroke, urgent revascularisation or acute stent thrombosis during 30 days of treatment with study drug. Enrollment commenced August 2011 and final results are expected September 2013. Conclusion: Study design, rationale and an Australian perspective will be presented.
