N2  - Purpose: Novel, potent P2Y12 ADP-receptor antagonists effectively may reduce risks for myocardial infarction and stent thrombosis compared to clopidogrel. However, their impact on stroke is unclear. We performed a meta-analysis to test whether new ADP-receptor inhibitors are better than clopidogrel in stroke prevention. Methods: Between 1996 and 2010, PUBMED and CENTRAL databases were searched for randomized, placebo-controlled cardiovascular trials that either compared A) clopidogrel with placebo or B) clopidogrel with a new-generation ADPreceptor antagonist in patients with ischemic heart disease (both acute coronary syndrome and stable angina) with the description of stroke rates during clinical follow-up. The included agents were clopidogrel, prasugrel, ticagrelor or cangrelor. Stroke was defined as a combination of both hemorrhagic and ischemic events. Since only randomized clinical trials were included, the outcome parameters were pooled utilizing the fixed-effect Mantel-Haenszel method. Results: Eleven studies comprising a total of 126,799 patients qualified for the overall analysis. Clopidogrel significantly reduced the occurrence of stroke compared to placebo (OR: 0.82, 95%CI: 0.72 - 0.93, P=0.002). In contrast, never antiplatelet agents had no incremental benefit on stroke compared to clopidogrel (OR: 1.12, 95%CI: 0.92 - 1.37, P=0.27). Importantly, the stroke outcomes exhibited no heterogeneity for clopidogrel vs. placebo: (I2: 0%, Chi2: 2.64, P=0.62); or clopidogrel vs. new antiplatelet agents (I2: 0%, Chi2: 1.18, P=0.95). Conclusion: Clopidogrel is effective in reducing the occurrence of stroke in patients with ischemic heart disease when compared to placebo. Lack of extra stroke prevention benefit for more potent antiplatelet regimens requires careful assessment of future cerebrovascular and bleeding risks.
