N2  - The adenosine diphosphate-P2Y12 interaction plays a critical role in amplifying platelet activation and is central to the genesis of coronary artery thrombosis. Despite the clinical efficacy of clopidogrel and aspirin therapy, post-stenting ischemic event occurrence remains a major concern. The pharmacodynamic effects of clopidogrel are characterized by the presence of high on-treatment platelet reactivity to ADP that is associated with post-stenting ischemic event occurrence. Prasugrel, the third generation thienopyridine, is associated with a rapid onset of action, greater active metabolite generation and greater platelet inhibition compared with clopidogrel. The superior clinical efficacy of prasugrel compared to clopidogrel demonstrated in the TRITON-TIMI 38 trial prompted its recommendation for the treatment of high risk ACS patients undergoing PCI. However, prasugrel is generally not recommended in patients < 60 kg body weight and ≥ 75 years old and is contraindicated in patients with a history of stroke or TIA. In stable coronary artery disease patients, ticagrelor (a new oral, reversibly binding, direct acting P2Y12 inhibitor, therapy), was associated with a more rapid onset of action, a greater level of inhibition, and a more rapid offset of pharmacodynamic action compared with clopidogrel. The demonstration of superior clinical efficacy of ticagrelor compared to clopidogrel in the PLATO trial lead to its recommendation in Europe to treat ACS patients. A significant reduction in mortality was a remarkable observation associated with ticagrelor therapy, but increased occurrence of dyspnea is a limitation. Elinogrel is an oral and parenteral, direct acting, novel P2Y12 receptor blocker associated with a fast onset and offset of action and greater platelet inhibition than clopidogrel. The newly developed P2Y12 receptor blockers offer significant advantages over clopidogrel for the acute and long-term treatment of patients with coronary artery disease.
