N2  - Background: Clopidogrel, an irreversible inhibitor of platelet P2Y12 ADP receptor, is widely used to reduce the risk of recurrent ischemic events. Despite its potent antiplatelet effects, clinical studies suggest that 10-30% of patients are resistant to therapy and it is not clear, which laboratory test is most suitable to identify these patients. Most studies include patients on combined clopidogrel and aspirin therapy. However, ADP induced platelet aggregation, which is widely used to test the effect of clopidogrel, is affected by aspirin and is not specific for P2Y12 receptor. Methods: Study population included 110 patients with the history of non-cardiogenic ischemic stroke being on clopidogrel monotherapy and 140 age and sex-matched controls. The effect of clopidogrel was tested by a newly developed P2Y12 receptor specific, modified platelet aggregation test in which prostaglandin E1 treated platelets are used. The test was compared with ADP induced platelet aggregation and secretion, Verify Now P2Y12 assay, flow cytometric analysis of vasodilator stimulated phosphoprotein (VASP) phosphorylation. For each method, diagnostic cut-offs were determined according to the guidelines of Clinical and Laboratory Standards Institute. Results: The ratio of clopidogrel non-responders varied between 10% and 54% depending on the method used. Approximately 50% of patients were found to be resistant by the traditional ADP aggregation and Verify Now tests. The newly developed method identified 25.9% of patients as non-responders, 32.6% as weak responders and 41.3% as responders. The best correlation was observed between this method and VASP phosphorylation (r = 0.81, P < 0.0001). The effect of aspirin was tested on the new method and no effect was observed. Conclusion: We have developed a new, reliable test for monitoring P2Y12 receptor inhibition by platelet aggregation. Monitoring clopidogrel therapy by adequate methods is essential as a subset of patients are non-responders.
