N2  - Antiplatelet drugs have been used to prevent aberrant platelet activation in pathophysiologic conditions such as ischemic stroke and acute coronary syndrome. The key role that ADP plays in this process has led to the development of antiplatelet drugs that inhibit its receptor P2Y12. Irreversible P2Y12 receptor antagonists, such as clopidogrel and prasugrel, have limited anti-thrombotic efficacy due to their bleeding risk. We have developed a novel orally active, reversible P2Y12 receptor antagonist, LC231306. The object of this study was to characterize the pharmacological properties of the LC231306, including in vitro and in vivo activity profiles of LC231306. In vitro activity of LC231306 is more potent than the other oral reversible P2Y12 receptor antagonist, Ticagrelor. LC231306 blocks the binding of 2MeSADP to platelets (IC50 = 23 nM. Ticagrelor IC50 = 34 nM). Also, It inhibits ADP-induced platelet aggregation in human platelet rich plasma (IC50 = 48 nM. Ticagrelor IC50 = 390 nM) and whole blood aggregation in human, dog and rat (IC50 = 18, 17 and 250 nM. Ticagrelor IC50 = 110, 130 and 490 nM), respectively. Effects of LC231306 on ex vivo whole blood aggregation and Arterio-Venous shunt model examined after oral administration to rats. Single oral dosing of LC231306 (5, 10, 30 mg/kg) to rats resulted in potent, dose-related inhibition of whole blood aggregation and thrombus formation. Also, single oral administration of LC231306 to dogs (0.1, 1, 5, 10 mg/kg) and multiple oral administration of LC231306 to dogs (1 and 5 mg/kg/day) once a day for 3 days resulted in more potent, dose-dependent and cumulative inhibition of ADP-induced whole blood aggregation than same dose of Ticagrelor. This study describes the pharmacological profiles of LC231306 having a novel orally active, reversible P2Y12 receptor antagonist could represent a new medical entity for platelet inhibition with a more potent efficacy than Ticagrelor.
