N2  - Background and Aim: As compared to Caucasians, East Asians more often carry CYP2C19 loss-of-function (LOF) allele with the CYP2C19∗3 variant. The impact of the CYP2C19∗3 allele on clopidogrel pharmacodynamics and clinical outcomes is unknown. We sought to evaluate the effect of the CYP2C19 gene variants on clopidogrel pharmacodynamics and long-term prognosis in East Asian patients with AMI. Methods: Patients who survived an AMI (n = 266) were enrolled in a single-center registry. Pre-discharge platelet reactivity was assessed with light transmittance aggregometry and the VerifyNow P2Y12 assay; CYP2C19∗2,∗3,∗17 and ABCB1 3435C>T variants were determined. The primary clinical endpoint was the composite of cardiovascular death, non-fatal MI, and ischemic stroke. Results: The median exposure to clopidogrel was 21 months (IQR 13-29). CYP2C19∗17 and ABCB1 3435C>T were not related to clopidogrel response or cardiovascular events. Platelet reactivity increased proportionally according to the number of CYP2C19 LOF alleles. In a multivariate regression analysis, carriage of two CYP2C19 LOF alleles only could predict the risk of high on-treatment platelet reactivity (HPR) (OR 3.9, 95% CI 1.5-10.3, P = 0.007); platelet reactivity and the rate of HPR did not differ between CYP2C19∗2 vs.∗3 allele. In addition, the CYP2C19 LOF allele carriage was an independent predictor of cardiovascular event occurrence; compared to non-carriers, carriers of one (HR 3.4, 95% CI 0.94-12.2, P = 0.062) and two CYP2C19 LOF allele(s) (HR 11.4, 95% CI 1.9-66.7, P = 0.008) were associated with clinical endpoint. The clinical impact of CYP2C19∗2 vs.∗3 allele was similar. Conclusions: Among East Asian patients who survived an AMI, CYP2C19 LOF allele carriage is a major determinant of clopidogrel pharmacodynamics and cardiovascular events; the influence of (Graph presented) CYP2C19∗2 and∗3 alleles on clopidogrel response and long-term outcomes is similar.
