N2  - The antiplatelet drugs play a crucial role in the management of thrombotic and cardiovascular disorders and are widely used globally. These drugs are useful in acute coronary syndromes, cerebrolvascular diseases such as stroke and peripheral arterial diseases. Aspirin exhibits polypharmacologic actions and has multiple targets besides modulating platelets. The novel antiplatelet drugs represent a variety of synthetic and biologic agents which include COX-1 and COX-2 inhibitors, Phosphodiesterase inhibitors, ADP receptor inhibitors, GPIIb/IIIa inhibitors, serotonin receptor inhibitors, PAR-I receptor inhibitors, Thromboxane receptor inhibitors and Omega III fatty acids. Thus, these drugs target specific sites on platelets to modulate their function. Aspirin is the oldest antiplatelet drug and targets COX-1 and COX-2 enzymes and many additional sites for its action are continually identified. Beside antiplatelet effects, this drug exhibits several other pharmacologic actions including analgesic, antiinflammatory and immunomodulatory actions. Aspirin resistance due to genomic variations in COX-1 and COX-2 have been noted in various populations. Ticlopidne was the first clinically used ADP receptor inhibitor which was followed by Clopidogrel. These two drugs have been initially used in ischemic disorders. After the landmark CAPRI trial, Clopidogrel is one of the most widely used cardiovascular drug. It has been widely tested in multiple clinical trials such as the CURE, CREDO, COMMIT, CHARISMA and many other trials for the cardiovascular indications. More recently, genomic variations have been noted in the clinical responses to Clopidogrel. Regardless of these issues Clopidogrel remains to be an important drug in cardiology. More recently newer ADP receptor inhibitors such as Prasugrel, Ticagrelor and Cangrelor have been clinically developed. Both the Prasugrel and Clopidogrel require endogenous activation whereas the Ticagrelor and Cangrelor do not require endogenous transformation. Thrombin receptor inhibitor targeting has also been used in the development of antiplatelet agents, however, safety issues have delayed the development of these agents. A recent example of this is the premature termination of vorapaxar, a PAR-I receptor inhibitor. The pharmacologic profiles of these drugs markedly differ in terms of pharmacokinetics, pharmacodynamics and population responses. Moreover the mechanisms of their actions and genetic variation in metabolism impact on both the safety and efficacy. This presentation will provide an update on the current developments in antiplatelet therapy and their impact on the management of cardiovascular disorders.
