N2  - Background/Aim: Although antithrombotic therapy plays a central role in the treatment of apoplexy, cardiac disorders and peripheral arterial diseases, recent studies suggest that the risk of upper gastrointestinal bleeding is increased by the concomitant use of anti-platelet drugs with the low dose aspirin. In the present study, we set up a screening model of gastric bleeding in rats using the lose dose aspirin and an anti-platelet drug clopidogrel, a P2Y12 receptor antagonist, and investigated the prophylactic effect of antiulcer drugs on gastric bleeding and lesion formation under such conditions. Methods: Male SD rats were used after 18 h fasting. Under urethane anesthesia, two catheters were inserted into the stomach, one from an incision in the esophagus and another through the pylorus via an incision in the duodenum. Then, the stomach was superfued with saline at a rate of 0.4 ml/min using an infusion pump, and the perfusate was collected every 5 min. After an equilibration period with saline perfusion for 60 min, the stomach was perfused with acidified aspirin (5-25 mM in 50 mM HCl) for another 60 min. Gastric bleeding was evaluated as the hemoglobin concentration in the perfusate. Clopidogrel (10-100 mg/kg, PO) was given 24 h before the perfusion. Several agents, including omeprazole (30 mg/kg), cimetidine (100 mg/kg), irsogradine (1-10 mg/kg), rebamipide (30 mg/kg), teprenone (300 mg/kg), egualene (30 mg/kg) or PGE2 (1 mg/kg) was given ID or IV, respectively, 80 min before the onset of aspirin perfusion, while was given 5 min before the aspirin perfusion. Results: Perfusion of the stomach with aspirin dose-dependently provoked gastric bleeding with a significant increase of hemoglobin content in the perfusate. Pretreatment with clopidogrel, although provoking by itself neither bleeding nor damage even at 100 mg/kg, dose-dependently increased the severity of gastric damage and bleeding caused by acidified aspirin. Irsogradine dose-dependently reduced the gastric bleeding caused by the acidified aspirin plus clopidogrel. Likewise, pretreatment with teprenone, rebamipide, egualene or PGE2 also significantly reduced the severity of gastric damage as well as bleeding in response to clopidogrel plus aspirin, while the antisecretory drugs had no effect. Conclusion: These results suggest that clopidogrel, the anti-platelet drug, increases the gastric bleeding induced by low dose aspirin. Mucosal protective drugs are useful for preventing gastric bleeding caused by aspirin plus clopidogrel. This model may be useful for screening of the protective drugs against gastric bleeding.
