N2  - Background: Recent studies have shown that the presence of genetic polymorphisms of CYP2C19 are responsible for decreased enzymatic activation of clopidogrel to its active metabolite, thereby reducing platelet inhibitory effect of clopidogrel. The CYP2C19∗2 allele, present in approximately 50% of Asians and 25% of Caucasians, is associated with decreased clopidogrel metabolism and platelet inhibition. It is not clear whether this decreased platelet inhibition increases major adverse cardiac events (MACE) in patients after percutaneous coronary intervention (PCI) with stent placement that are prescribed clopidogrel. We investigated outcomes after PCI in Asians and Caucasians patient populations to determine whether MACE rates were different in these ethnic groups. Their PCI outcome analysis was thought to either support or dispute the clinical impact of CYP2C19∗2 allele heterogeneity. Methods: This is a retrospective cohort analysis of clinical outcomes from a single center cardiac catheterization laboratory with reference to the New York State Coronary PCI database. Data of 30-day rates of mortality, stent thrombosis, myocardial infarction, stroke, and target vessel revascularization (MACE) were collected and reviewed among the Asian and Caucasian patients who underwent PCI. The analysis included 3,529 patients (1266 Asian and 2263 Caucasian) who underwent PCI at our institution between 8/1/2005 and 5/28/2010. All patients received clopidogrel following coronary stent placement. Results: Caucasian patients had a significantly higher rate of MACE with 41 events (1.81%) compared to Asians with 10 events (0.28%) (P=0.0147). Backwards selection logistic regression analysis demonstrated that older age (P=0.0024) and Caucasian race (P=0.046) were significant independent predictors of MACE within 30 days following PCI and stent placement. Conclusions: Asian ethnicity was associated with lower 30 day MACE rates than anticipated following coronary stent placement. This analysis does not support the hypothesis that the increased prevalence of the CYP2C19∗2 allele in Asians may lead to higher MACE due to decreased clopidogrel metabolism and platelet inhibition.
