N2  - Antiplatelet therapy with aspirin and clopidogrel (Plavix®) decreases the incidence of secondary myocardial infarction, stroke, peripheral artery disease and cardiovascular death in vascular patients. Aspirin inhibits platelet function by acetylating cytoplasmic cyclooxygenase and clopidogrel (Plavix®) blocks the membrane adenosine diphosphate (ADP) receptor P2Y12. While they are effective as a class of therapy, many patients continue to experience adverse events despite therapy with these agents. Aspirin inhibits platelet aggregation to the agonist arachidonic acid and clopidogrel inhibits aggregation to ADP. Many patients have been described with a suboptimal response to these drugs, with demonstration of residual platelet reactivity (RPR), sometimes call drug “resistance”. There have been many laboratory techniques devised to measure the effect of antiplatelet drugs, including light transmittance aggregometry, whole blood impedance aggregometry, PFA-100, Accumetrics VerifyNow, urine-based 11-dehydro thromboxane B2, and VASP phosphorylation. However, a “gold standard” does not exist and there is little standardization of functional testing for RPR. Meta-analyses have demonstrated suboptimal response to aspirin in 27.1% (95% CI 21.5-32.6) of patients, with a suboptimal response to clopidogrel in 22% (95% CI 15-29) of cases. However, there is little agreement among the laboratory tests, with only 2% of subjects identified as suboptimal response by all. Suboptimal response is more than a laboratory phenomenon, as meta-analyses have correlated aspirin RPR in vascular disease patients to increased risk of stroke, acute myocardial infarction, and cardiovascular death (OR 3.78; 95% CI 2.34-6.11), and others have documented adverse vascular events in clopidogrel RPR (OR 8.00; 95% CI 3.36-19.06. Indeed, RPR has been consistently linked to adverse outcomes using various platforms despite poor inter-platform agreement. A number of genetic studies have identified potential causes for antiplatelet resistance, including membrane receptor P2Y12 polymorphisms and cytochrome P450 polymorphisms CYP2C19, CYP3A4, CYP3A5, CYP1A2 and CYP2B6. The best documented genomic association for clopidogrel is CYP2C19 ∗2, which leads to poor clopidogrel metabolism to the active form and has been shown to be associated with RPR, major adverse cardiovascular events and stent thrombosis. An additional CYP2C19 polymorphism (∗17) is associated with an increased response to clopidogrel and bleeding risk. Several platforms are now available to perform CYP2C19 genotyping in the laboratory. Testing strategies for clopidgrel have included both functional and genomic assays. A genomic profile may identify some proportion of risk, but this may change over time with the clinical scenario and other drug therapy, so platelet function testing is likely to provide more complete therapeutic information. At present, there is little evidence to guide therapy in patients with RPR. The recently completed GRAVITAS trial of 5429 patients undergoing PCI showed no benefit of doubling the dose in clopidogrel non-responders. Current efforts are directed towards the possibilities of dual antiplatelet drug therapy; alternative P2Y12 antagonists, such as prasugrel and ticagrelor; or the addition of current antithrombotic drugs such as dipyridamole of cilostazole. Small randomized studies studies support the benefit of tailored therapy, but will have to be confirmed in large clinical trials. Answers and recommendations for an appropriate testing and therapeutic regimen are anticipated upon completion of these clinical trials.
