N2  - Objectives: Clopidogrel is an antithrombotic drug prescribed to treat acute coronary syndromes, myocardial infarction, stroke and peripheral arterial disease. Clopidogrel administered as a pro-drug is largely (∼85%) hydrolyzed by esterases into inactive metabolites, and only about ∼15% is first converted into active metabolite by hepatic Cytochrome P450 (CYP) enzymes, primarily by CYP2C19. The active metabolite is an antagonist to platelet ADP receptor P2RY12 involved in thrombosis. Formation of active metabolite and hence, therapeutic response to clopidogrel is reportedly associated with the variants of CYP2C19 gene. In fact, a wide variability to clopidogrel therapy exists, and up to 30% of patients do not benefit from the therapy. Variants CYP2C19∗2 and CYP2C19∗3 have been associated with non-responsiveness to clopidogrel, while CYP2C19∗17 is associated with increased response to clopidogrel and risk for bleeding. Accordingly, CYP2C19 genotyping of patients for common variants ∗2, ∗3 and ∗17 can help in predicting the metabolic status to determine the appropriate therapy including tailored dosage of clopidogrel. Methods: Genomic DNA isolated from 54 specimens from research studies that were previously genotyped for CYP2C19∗2, ∗3, ∗4 and ∗17 by a custom-made Illumina 50 K SNP array and TaqMan methods, and 15 DNAs of known CYP2C19 genotypes from Coriell Institute were analytically validated by genotyping with a commercial platform INFINITI from AutoGenomics, Inc. The INFINITI employs an array-based assay (CYP2C19+) capable of genotyping 9 different genetic variants of CYP2C19 including ∗2-∗4, ∗6-∗10 and ∗17 simultaneously. For analytical validation, we have determined accuracy, method comparison, intra- and inter-assay precision, sensitivity and specificity of the CYP2C19 + assay. Results: Accuracy of the CYP2C19+ assay, determined by comparing the consistency in genotyping calls between the known and observed results, was in total agreement for all four variants of research specimens (n=54) and the known variants of Coriell DNAs (n=15). A subset of specimens (n=26) genotyped in an external reference laboratory using the same INFINITI platform for CYP2C19+ assay yielded concordant results. Three specimens representing extensive (EM), intermediate (IM) and poor (PM) metabolizers genotyped in six replicates within the same run (intra-assay) and in duplicates over five days (interassay) yielded reproducible results without any discrepancy. Sensitivity of the CYP2C19+ assay determined in duplicates using one each of EM, IM and PM demonstrated that accurate genotype calls could be generated with DNA amount ranging from 3 ng (1/16thx) to 100 ng (2x) per reaction compared to the recommended level of 50 ng DNA per reaction (1x). The assay was specific as there was no false positive or false negative, the template-free control remained negative and the hybridization and background controls met the assay criteria. Conclusions: Pharmacogenetic testing of CYP2C19 using the INFINITY platform from AutoGenomics has been analytically validated with satisfactory results. Performance evaluation for accuracy, precision, sensitivity and specificity has shown that CYP2C19+ assay is reliable and can be easily adapted to genotype patients to predict their response to clopidogrel therapy. Although this assay can genotype nine different variants encompassing multiethnic groups, clinical utility of many of the ethnocentric variants are yet to be established.
