N2  - Aims: While acute MI is associated with impaired clopidogrel responsiveness, systematic evaluation is lacking due to the inability of functional aggregation-based assays to analyse clopidogrel responsiveness in the presence of glycoprotein IIb/IIIa inhibitors. Using the P2Y12-specific, non-aggregation-based platelet-reactivity-index (PRI) we assessed clopidogrel responsiveness in patients with acute myocardial infarction (MI) Methods and results: Clopidogrel responsiveness was determined 24 hours after loading with 600mg in 54 patients widi acute MI admitted for coronary intervention. A pre-defined cut-off PRI >50% was considered as suboptimal inhibition based on previous studies. Clinical outcome was assessed as a combination of cardiac death, non-fatal MI, stent thrombosis, ischemic stroke, and urgent target vessel revascularisation after 12 months. Overall response in MI patients was suboptimal with a median PRI of 58%. Diabetes, low HDL and prehospital clopidogrel loading were associated with impaired clopidogrel responsiveness. Patients loaded at first medical contact had a significantly weaker platelet inhibition by clopidogrel after 24 hours (PRI 63%) compared to those loaded peri-interventionally (PRI 54%, p=0.014). The majority of the clinical events occurred in the two quartilcs with the highest PRI's. The pre-selected cut-off of PRI ≤50% yielded a sensitivity of 87% at a specificity of 26%, whereas a PRI ≤57% determined by ROC-analysis yielded a sensitivity of 80% at a specificity of 56% (event rate: PRI≤57%- 12.0%; PRI>57%: 41.4%, p=0.0136). The combined clinical endpoint occurred significantly more often in acute MI patients with a PRI > 57% than in patients with a PRI ≤ 57% (12.% vs. 41.4%; HR 4.3; 95% CI 1.3 to 9.9, p=0.0136) and more often in MI patients receiving the clopidogrel loading dose prehospitally than patients treated periinterventionally (5.3% vs. 43.7%; HR 10 8; 95% CI 1.63 to 12.6; p=0.0038). Conclusions: PRI detects clopidogrel responsiveness in acute MI patients requiring glycoprotein IIb/IIIa antagonism; and impaired clopidogrel responsiveness predisposes to clinical events. Prehospital clopidogrel loadmg was associated with impaired response and more adverse events challenging the concept of earliest oral clopidogrel loading in acute MI patients.
