N2  - Background: Clopidogrel is a prodrug requiring a cytochrome CYP2C19-dependent conversion to become active. Published data regarding the effect of concomitant clopidogrel and proton pump inhibitor (PPI) therapy on cardiovascular outcomes have been conflicting. Methods: We performed a meta-analysis of studies in Pubmed, Ovid, ISI Science, Embase, and meeting abstracts. Primary analysis was based on definite vascular outcomes including all cause mortality, cardiac death, myocardial infarction, and/or stroke. Secondary analysis also incorporated probable cardiac events which included re-hospitalization for cardiac symptoms or revascularization procedures. Risk differences (RD) were obtained for studies with definite and probable endpoints and were combined using a random-effects model. Results: We reviewed 1204 publications of which 26 studies (15 published articles, 11 abstracts) met inclusion criteria. The meta-analysis that compared clopidogrel with clopidogrel + PPI showed a risk difference of 3% (95% CI 95% CI 2-4%) for the 15 published articles and 2% (95% CI 1-4%) for all 26 studies (Table and Figure). The meta-analysis for secondary outcomes yielded a risk difference of 2% (95% CI 0-3%) based on 12 published papers, and 2% (95% CI 1-4%) based on 18 published papers and abstracts. When primary and secondary outcomes were combined, the meta-analysis yielded a risk difference of 4% (95% CI 3-6%). Conclusions: In patients using concomitant clopidogrel and PPI therapy, the risk of adverse cardiac outcomes appears to be increased by approximately 2-3%. Addition of probable vascular events increased the risk difference estimates. (Table presented).
