N2  - Purpose: Certain genetic and environmental factors may interfere with the clopidogrel treatment outcomes in western population of pts with acute ACS, particularly in those who undergo PCI. Limited information exists on the risks associated with impaired pharmacogenetics of clopidogrel and possible drug interactions in pts who undergo elective PCI and in pts of different race/ethnicity. Aim of the study was to assess the impact of reduced-efficacy polymorphisms (ABCB1; CYP2C19+2) and concomitant use of PPI on long-term prognosis after elective PCI in Russian pts with CAD. Methods: the study population included 399 pts, mean age 59,4 yrs, presenting with stable CAD and receiving clopidogrel (75-150 mg od) mostly due to recent elective PCI. We assessed the relation of allelic variants (ABCB1 C3435T and CYP2C19±2) and use of PPI to the risk of vascular events (VE) (cardiovascular death, ACS, stroke/TIA, need for coronary revascularization) during exposure to clopidogrel. Results: Of the pts included, 291 (72,9%) were CYP2C19 wild-type carriers ∗1/∗1; 101 (25,3%) were heterozygotes ∗1/∗2 and 7 (1,8%) were homozygotes ∗2/∗2. ABCB1 wild genotype (CC) was detected in 106 (26,6%) pts, CT - in 220 (55,1%) and TT - in 73 (18,3%) pts. Mean clopidogrel exposure time was 18 months. VE occurred in 70 (17,5%) of pts during this period. ABCB1 polymorphism was not associated with the risk of adverse outcome. Higher event rate was found for both CYP2C19 ∗2/∗2 and ∗1/∗2 carriers, compared with wild-type homozygotes, 51,7% and 17,8% vs 16,3%; adjusted RR 4,9 (95% CI 1,7-13,9, p=0.003) and 1,5 (95% CI 0,8-2,7) respectively. Concomitant intake of PPI (mostly omeprazole) was selfreported in 72 (18%) of pts. VE occurred in 16,5% of pts not taking PPI and in 22,2 % of pts taking PPI, adjusted RR 1,8 (95% CI 1,0-3,4). Among pts receiving PPI there was a tendency to the worth outcomes in CYP2C19 wild type carriers compared with heterozygotes ∗1/∗2, adjusted RR 0,2 (95% CI 0,02-1,5). Conclusion: CYP2C19±2 and ABCB1 genotypes distribution in Russian pts was comparable with other Caucasian populations. CYP2C19 ∗2/∗2 and use of PPI are independent risk factors of VE following elective PCI. We suggest that PPI use reduces clopidorel efficacy in CYP2C19 wild type carriers but not in heterozygotes ∗1/∗2.
