N2  - Background: Angioplasty and stenting carries a risk of cerebral thrombo-embolic events. Dual antiplatelet therapy with clopidogrel plus aspirin has been used to reduce ischemic event following percutaneous catheter intervention. Platelet reactivity studies have demonstrated a wide inter-individual variability in the inhibitory response to clopidogrel. Objectives: To determine the relationship between the poor response to clopidogrel and peri-procedural complications such as in-stent thrombosis and cerebral embolism following stent-assisted angioplasty. Methods: One hundred two patients, who have received stent-assisted angioplasty procedures in extracranial carotid (n=55), intracranial ICA (n= 12), MCA (n= 8), intracranial vertebro-basilar arteries (n=18), and vertebral ostium (n= 14) were enrolled. Before the intervention, all patients were on aspirin and received a loading dose of 300mg clopidogrel. The platelet function was determined with the VerifyNow-P2Y12 assay. This assay reports the results as P2Y12 Reaction Units (PRU), Inhibition (%) and BASE in less than 5 minutes. Inhibition (%) is calculated as (1-PRU/BASE) ×100. Diffusion-weighted image (DWI) was performed the day before and repeated within 48 hours after the procedure to screening for procedure-related ischemic lesions, including silent ischemic embolism. Results: DWI detected procedure-related embolic lesions in 44 (41.1%). Most lesions were asymptomatic, except in 3 patients showing transient weakness and sensory change. In-stent thrombosis on angiography during the procedure was found in 11 (32%), which developed only in intracranial arteries (ICA, n=3; MCA, n=3; BA, n=2). Patients with positive DWI lesion had higher mean value of PRU than ones without DWI lesion (241.4±73.1 vs. 205.7±93.1, p=0032). The number of DWI lesions correlated with the value of PRU (r=0.57; P<0.01). In 38 patients with intracranial angioplasty and stenting the suboptimal responder to clopidogrel (percentage of Inhibition < 20) was more often found in patients showing in-stent thrombosis than in ones without (7/11 vs. 6/27, p=0.024).Conclusion: Clopidogrel-induced platelet inhibition before angioplasty and stenting may correlate with early thrombo-embolic complications following the intervention. The further studies will be required to determine the prognostic value of the rapid platelet function assay.
