N2  - Evidence-based guidelines have been published by a variety of organizations regarding prevention of recurrent stroke. Anticoagulants are recommended to reduce the risk of a recurrent stroke for survivors of cardiomebolic stroke, but have not been found to be better than antiplatelet agents for non-cardioembolic stroke. Current antiplatelet options after non-cardioembolic stroke include aspirin (ASA, 50 mg to 325 mg/day), the combination of low-dose aspirin and extended-release dipyridamole (ASA + ER-DP), and clopidogrel (CP) alone. New antithrombotic options are undergoing evaluation. In the setting of acute TIA or minor ischemic stroke, the POINT (Platelet-Oriented Inhibition in New TIA) trial is evaluating clopidogrel and aspirin compared with aspirin. The platelet P2Y12 receptor is a target for developing antiplatelet agents, such as the thienopyridines (ticlopidine and clopidogrel). Due to the relatively slow onset of action and issues regarding clopidogrel resistance, other novel P2Y12 antagonists have been under investigation. These include: (i) prasugrel, an orally-administered thienopyridine prodrug; (ii) ticagrelor (AZD6140), an ATP analog reversible P2Y12 antagonist; (iii) cangrelor, an intravenously-administered reversible P2Y12 antagonist; (iv) PRT060128. These are being evaluated in a number of settings which may eventually include stroke. New alternatives to traditional Vitamin K antagonists are also being tested for patients with cardioembolic TIA or ischemic stroke. Dabigatran etexilate, a direct thrombin inhibitor, has demonstrated efficacy compared to warfarin. Other new anticoagulants, including the oral factor Xa inhibitors, rivaroxaban, apixaban, and edoxaban, the parenteral factor Xa inhibitor, idrabiotaparinux, and the novel VKA, tecarfarin, are currently being assessed.
