N2  - Background and aims: Silent cerebral ischemic lesions after carotid artery stenting are frequently observed after procedure. We investigated the correlation between anti-platelet resistance and silent ischemia developed after carotid artery stenting. Methods: We selected 56 patients performed carotid artery stenting and brain MRI 24 h after stenting. Risk factors, laboratory findings, anti-platelet resistance for aspirin or clopidogrel and cytochrome P450 2C19 polymorphism were investigated. We analyzed the difference between no ischemia group and silent ischemia group on 24 h diffusion weighted MRI. Results: Silent ischemic lesions were observed in 14 patients (25%), 9 ipsilateral, 1 contralateral, and 4 bilateral. Among 56 patients, anti-platelet resistance was evaluated in 38 patients for aspirin and 36 patients for clopidogrel. Aspirin resistance was observed in 8 patients and Clopidogrel resistance in 17 patients. Clopidogrel resistance was observed in 12 of 14 silent ischemia group, compared to 5 of 22 no ischemia group (P = 0.001). Aspirin resistance was observed in 3 of 14 silent ischemia group, compared to 5 of 24 no ischemia group (P = 0.965). Cytochrome P450 2C19 were evaluated in 10 patients. Poor and intermediate metabolizer observed in 5 of 14 silent ischemia group, compared to 2of 42 of no ischemia group. Conclusions: Anti-platelet resistance for clopidogrel is associated with silent ischemia after carotid arterial stenting. Evaluation of anti-platelet resistance, especially clopidogrel may be useful to define high risk group when performing carotid artery stenting.
