N2  - Background and aims: The inhibitory response to clopidogrel considerably varies among individuals and clopidogrel resistance is a risk factor for thrombotic events in patients with cardiovascular disease. The present study investigates clopidogrel resistance and the effect of adding cilostazol on platelet aggregation assayed using Verify Now P2Y12 Assay. Methods: We examined 77 patients (53 men and 24 women, aged 65.8 ± 9.9 years) with ischemic stroke or cerebral artery stenosis who received clopidogrel (75 mg) for >7 days at our hospital between October 2009 and March 2010. We administered 62 (42 men and 20 women, aged 65.3 ± 9.9 years) of them with 75 mg clopidogrel alone (monotherapy) and the other 15 (11 men and 4 women, aged 67.9 ± 9.9 years) received 75 mg of clopidogrel plus 100 or 200 mg of cilostazol (combination). We measured the ability of 20 lM ADP to aggregate platelets using the VerifyNow P2Y12 Assay. Clopidogrel resistance was defined as ≤10% inhibition in this assay. Results: Clopidogrel resistance was identified in 10 (16.1%) of the 62 patients in the monotherapy group. The% inhibition was significantly higher in the combination, than in the monotherapy group (41.7 ± 28.0% vs. 64.9 ± 22.7%, P = 0.005). None of the patients in the combination group had ≤10% inhibition. Conclusions: Clopidogrel resistance developed in 16.1% of patients given clopidogrel alone. Adding cilostazol to clopidogrel intensified platelet inhibition.
