N2  - Background and aims: Clopidogrel is frequently used to achieve sustained platelet inhibition in high-risk patients with ischemic stroke, who are often also treated with statins. The aim of this study is to evaluate the influence of co-treatment with atorvastatin on the inhibition of platelet activation by clopidogrel in ischemic stroke patients. Methods: Seven patients (5 male, 2 female; 73 ± 14 years old) with both chronic cerebral infarction and dyslipidemia were recruited. We (Figure presented). detected activated platelet markers PAC-1 (monoclonal antibody to fibrinogen receptors) and anti-CD62P (monoclonal antibody to P-selectin), as well as WBC-platelet complex, by flow cytometry. Blood samples in patients receiving 75 mg/day clopidogrel were taken before, and at 2 weeks and 2 months after the start of administration of 10 mg/day atorvastatin. Results: Treatment with atorvastatin for 2 months significantly reduced LDL from 143 ± 32 to 87 ± 17 mg/dL. MoAb-CD62P positive platelets were significantly reduced by clopidogrel+atorvastatin for 2 months, compared with clopidogrel alone (Figure). No significant difference in PAC-1-positive platelets or WBC-platelet complex was observed between clopidogrel alone and clopidogrel+atorvastatin. Conclusions: Our results indicate that inhibition of platelet activation by clopidogrel was promoted by co-treatment with atorvastatin. A possible mechanism would be translocation of P2Y12 outside the lipid raft by atorvastatin.
