N2  - Background/Aims: High on-treatment platelet reactivity is associated with thrombo-ischemic events following percutaneous coronary intervention with stent implantation. The primary aim of this study is to evaluate the capability of multiple platelet function tests to predict clinical outcome. Methods: The POPular-study enrolled 1069 consecutive patients on clopidogrel- and aspirin therapy undergoing elective PCI. On-treatment platelet reactivity was measured in parallel by light transmittance aggregometry (LTA), the VerifyNow® P2Y12 assay, the Plateletworks® assay, the IMPACT-R and the PFA-100® System (COL/ADP as well as the novel INNOVANCE P2Y∗ cartridge ). Clinical follow-up was obtained after 12 months. The primary endpoint was defined as all-cause death, nonfatal MI, stent thrombosis and ischemic stroke. The primary safety endpoint was defined as major or minor bleeding (TIMI). Cut-off values for High on-treatment Platelet Reactivity (HPR) were established by receiver-operator characteristic (ROC) curve analysis. Results: LTA, VerifyNow® and Plateletworks® were able to discriminate between patients with and without primary endpoint. At one-year follow-up, the primary endpoint occurred more frequently in patients with HPR when assessed by LTA (11.7% vs 6.0%, p=0.0009 [n=1049]), VerifyNow (13.3% vs 5.7%, p<0.0001 [n=1052)] and Plateletworks (12.6% vs 6.1%, p=0.005 [n=606]). In contrast, the IMPACT-R (n=905), the PFA Collagen/ADP (n=812) and INNOVANCE® PFA P2Y∗ (n=588) were unable to discriminate between patients with and without primary endpoint at one-year follow-up. None of the platelet function tests were able to identify patients at risk for bleeding. Conclusions: Of the platelet function tests assessed only LTA, VerifyNow® and Plateletworks® were significantly associated with the primary endpoint, with only modest predictability. The platelet function tests were unable to predict bleedings. At this moment, the POPular-study does not support the use of platelet function testing to guide clinical practice in a low-risk population of patients undergoing elective PCI.
