N2  - Rationale: Low dose aspirin (ASA) is evidenced as effective management in acute ischaemic stroke ineligible for intravenous thrombolysis (IV rt-PA). Reported efficacy is due to antiplatelet properties. Clopidogrel is anADP-P2Y12 receptor antagonist with a characteristic higher dose dependent onmore intense platelet inhibition&earlier onset. Subjects: Patients ineligible for IV rt-PA, aged 18-75, with definite onset of first clinical ischaemic stroke within 8 hours and admission NIHSS between 4-24; excluding those with potential bleeding tendencies. Methods: First phase is an open-label pilot observational safety monitoring study. Patients will be given on admission 600mg clopidogrel then 150mg ASA daily, followed up clinically (NIHSS) and by imaging (2nd day MRA & MRI: T1W, T2W, DWI, FLAIR, T2∗, ≈l7th day DWI, FLAIR, T2∗). Proceeding into the next phase will take place after safety ensuring. The main phase will be a randomized doubleblinded ASA controlled study (Group A: ASA 300mg, C: 600mg clopidogrel within 8 hours of onset, followed in both by dailyASA 150mg). NIHSS is the clinical&plain CT brain is the imaging parameter for follow-up. Further subgroup analysis might indicate an escalating dose or time window tapering in an intention-to-treat final phase of CAIRO. Endpoints: Symptomatic intracerebral haemorrhage with NIHSS drop of more than 4 is the primary end point for the introductory phase. Secondary endpoints include GIT, mucous membrane & peripheral bleedings and improvement in NIHSS 4 on day 3, or 50%of admission score by the first week.
