N2  - urpose: The purpose of this study was to investigate the impact of hyporesponsiveness to aspirin and clopidogrel designated by VerifyNow Assay on early outcomes of patients after percutaneous coronary intervention (PCI). Method: A total of 301 consecutive patients (mean age 62.3 years) undergoing PCI were enrolled. All patients were pretreated with aspirin (100 mg/d) and clopidogrel (75 mg/d) at least 5 days or received loading doses of aspirin 250 mg and clopidogrel 300 mg before PCI. Blood samples were obtained in catheterization room before PCI and evaluated with VerifyNow Aspirin and P2Y12. All patients were clinically followed for 30 days to assess MACE, defined as death, myocardial infarction, stroke, and target vessel revascularization. Results: VerifyNow Assay discriminated patients with and without 30-day MACE by arachidonic acid-induced and adenosine diphosphate-induced platelet reactivity (area under the curve [AUC] 0.606, p=0.049; AUC 0.668, p=0.002). Aspirin reaction units (ARU) value 419 was identified as the optimal cutoff point to predict 30-day outcome with 78% sensitivity and 43% specificity. Likewise, P2Y12 reaction units (PRU) value 310 was optimal with 81% sensitivity and 53% specificity. Based on these cutoff values, patients with reduced responses to both drugs had significantly higher prevalence of MACE (Fig). Multivariate analysis revealed dual hyporesponsiveness and total stent length (mm) were independent predictors of 30-day MACE with odds ratio, 4.92 (95% CI 2.1 ∼ 11.4, p<0.001) and 1.02 (95% CI 1.01∼1.04, p=0.012), respectively. (GP.) Conclusion: Dual hyporesponsiveness measured by VerifyNow Assay was an independent predictor of early outcomes after PCI.
