N2  - Background: P2Y12 inhibition by clopidogrel is the standard treatment in patients following percutaneous coronary interventions and/or myocardial infarction (MI). Current guidelines recommend the administration of a loading dose as early as possible. However, acute coronary syndrome has been reported as a risk factor for impaired responsiveness to clopidogrel. We tested the effectiveness of prehospital clopidogrel loading in ST-segment elevation MI (STEMI) patients. Methods: Blood was collected from 54 patients with acute STEMI admitted for urgent coronary intervention. 50 patients with stable coronary artery disease were used as controls. Responsiveness to clopidogrel was assessed 24 hours after the administration of the loading dose using the P2Y12-specific platelet-reactivityindex (PRI). 33 STEMI patients had received the loading dose at first medical contact outside the intervention centre, 21 STEMI patients received the loading dose peri-interventional at the catheterization laboratory. A PRI >50% was considered as a suboptimal inhibition of P2Y12. Results: Overall response was impaired in STEMI patients vs. controls (PRI [Mean±SEM]: STEMI 58±2%, Control 43±2%, p< 0.0001). Patients loaded at first medical contact had a significantly weaker platelet inhibition by clopidogrel 24 hours following loading (PRI 62±3%) compared to those loaded in the catheterization laboratory (PRI 53±3%, p=0.0079). Patients receiving clopidogrel loading prehospitally and displaying a PRI > 50% had a worse outcome defined by a composite end-point of death, cardiac arrest, recurrent myocardial infarction, stroke, and cardiovascular rehospitalisation (p=0.022 PRI≤50% vs. PRI>50%; p=0.0177 prehospital vs. periinterventional). Conclusion: Application of clopidogrel at pre-hospital conditions in STEMI patients is associated with impaired responsiveness and higher rates of suboptimal platelet inhibition. This was associated with increased cardiovascular events.
