N2  - Background: Elevated plasma levels of succinate, a citric acid cycle intermediate, are found in various pathologic conditions characterized by severe mitochondrial dysfunction due to hypoxic organ damage such as stroke, acute myocardial infarc- tion, sepsis and cancer. Using a novel and highly sensitive microarray technique, we have previously confirmed the presence of the succinate receptor (GPR91) mRNA in human platelets. Methods: Washed platelets from healthy donors were used to assess the effect of succinate on platelet aggregation under low shear stress. Surface CD62P (P-selectin) expression following incubation of diluted platelet-rich plasma with succinate under static conditions was analyzed by flow cytometry. Results: Stimulation with high concentrations of succinate alone (1000 μM) resulted in delayed and variable platelet aggregation, suggesting functional dependence of the platelet response on concomitantly activated signaling pathways. Consequently, low concentrations of succinate (10-100 μM) consistently enhanced platelet aggregation in response to exogenous ADP. Furthermore, the inhibitory effect of small-molecule P2Y1 and P2Y12 antagonists on ADP-induced platelet aggregation was reversed when platelets were pre-incubated with succinate (100 μM). Similarly, while succinate alone had negligible effects on surface CD62P expression, it dose-dependently enhanced the platelet response to ADP and partially abrogated the inhibitory effects of ADP receptor antagonists. Discussion: Our findings suggest that extracellular succinate may have a costimulatory role in platelet thrombus formation under conditions of severe organ ischemia and tissue damage, possibly via activation of its membrane surface receptor, GPR91. In addition, variations in endogenous succinate plasma levels may contribute to the inter-individual variability commonly observed in platelet function testing and to the still incompletely understood phenomenon of clopidogrel resistance.
