N2  - Background: Antiplatelet agents are widely used for secondary prevention of ischemic stroke. The use of clopidogrel, the combination of aspirin and clopidogrel (ASA+ clopidogrel), and the combination of aspirin and dipyridamole (ASA+dipyridamole) have been increasing as alternatives to aspirin (ASA). However, effectiveness of these drugs may be different by race and genetic polymorphism. Objectives: To compare each antiplatelet agents' effectiveness for secondary prevention of stroke in Asian population. Methods: A retrospective cohort study was conducted using the Korean Health Insurance Review and Assessment Service (HIRA) database. We selected elderly patients received outpatient antiplatelet prescriptions after hospitalization for ischemic stroke. Then the study subjects were followed up to see rehospitalization due to stroke recurrence. Cox proportional hazard model was constructed to assess the relationship between prescribed antiplatelet therapy and incidence of ischemic stroke recurrence after antiplatelet therapy while adjusting for age, gender and comorbidities. Results: A total of 40,761 patients were prescribed ASA, clopidogrel or dipyridamole after hospital discharge (mean age 73.7 years, and female 58.5%). 23,890 patients received ASA, 7,536 patients received clopidogrel, 8,999 patients received ASA+clopidogrel and 244 patients received ASA+dipyridamole. Compared with patients taking ASA, the adjusted hazard ratio of stroke recurrence was 1.44 (95% CI: 1.34-1.54) in patients taking clopidogrel, 0.94 (95% CI: 0.87-1.02) in patients taking ASA+clopidogrel and 1.25 (95% CI: 0.89-1.76) in patients taking ASA+dipyridamole. Conclusions: Compared with ASA, ASA+clopidogrel and ASA+dipyridamole showed no clinically significant difference in effectiveness for secondary prevention of stroke, but clopidogrel only was less effective. Further study for adjusting stroke severity will be needed.
