N2  - Drug treatment for cardiovascular disease accounts for about 20% of all drug costs. Despite this outlay, there is clinically important variability in response to drug therapy, and an increasing body of knowledge links this variability to rare and common DNA polymorphisms. Genetic variation in cardiovascular therapy has been linked to both efficacy and adverse effects. One key finding across studies has been variation in response by ancestry, and genetic variation underlying this phenomenon is now being identified. Single CYP2C19 loss of function variants with large effects have been convincingly associated with altered bioactivation of the P2Y12 receptor antagonist clopidogrel, and this translates into a higher incidence of cardiovascular events such as MI, stroke, or death in patients with variant CYP2C19. Variable warfarin dosage requirements are associated with variants both in the drug metabolism pathway (CYP2C9) and in the target protein with which warfarin interacts (VKORC1). Genetic variation conferring susceptibility to serious rare adverse events, such as statin-induced myopathy and drug-induced torsades de pointes, has been identified in both candidate gene and unbiased experimental approaches. These new findings and methods to potentially apply this rapidly expanding knowledge base to the bedside will be discussed.
