N2  - Background: The prevalence of ex vivo antiplatelet “non-responsiveness” in the late phase after TIA/ischaemic stroke is uncertain. Methods: Cross-sectional study to simultaneously assess platelet function in 42 patients on Aspirin 75mg/d & Dipyridamole MR 200mg bd combination therapy, and 23 on Clopidogrel 75mg/d ≥ 3 months after TIA/ischaemic stroke. Inhibition of platelet function was assessed (i) at high shear stress with the PFA-100, which exposes platelets to stimulation with collagen + ADP (C-ADP) or epinephrine (CEPI), and (ii) at low shear stress with the VerifyNow aggregometry system (Aspirin and P2Y12 cartridges). (i) On the PFA-100, ex vivo antiplatelet non-responsiveness was defined as failure to prolong the closure time > mean +2SD of our control range on the (a) C-EPI cartridge with Aspirin/Dipyridamole > 167s, and (b) C-ADP with Clopidogrel > 137s. (ii) On the VerifyNow (a) Aspirin non-responsiveness was defined by Aspirin Reaction Units (ARU) ≥ 550 on the Aspirin cartridge; (b) Clopidogrel non-responsiveness was defined as P2Y12 reaction units (PRU) ≥ 418, as per manufacturer's literature. Results: (i) With PFA-100, 17/42 patients (40.1%) on Aspirin/Dipyridamole were non-responsive on the C-EPI cartridge. 21/23 patients (91.3%) on Clopidogrel were non-responsive on the C-ADP cartridge. (ii) With VerifyNow, 3/42 (7%) Aspirin/Dipyridamole patients were non-responsive on the Aspirin cartridge. No Clopidogrel-treated patient was non-responsive on the P2Y12 cartridge (mean PRU 205; range 35-372). Inter-device concordance in the definition of nonresponsiveness was low for Aspirin/Dipyridamole (16%, κ = 0.33), and zero for clopidogrel. Discussion: Our study reveals a much higher prevalence of antiplatelet nonresponsiveness with the “high shear stress” PFA-100 than with the “low shear stress” VerifyNow analyser. Longitudinal studies in individuals with more robust definitions of ex vivo non-responsiveness are warranted to assess their clinical predictive value.
