N2  - Background/Aim: Although antithrombotic therapy plays a central role in the treatment of apoplexy, cardiac disorders and peripheral arterial diseases, recent studies suggest that the risk of upper gastrointestinal bleeding is increased by the concomitant use of anti-platelet drugs with a low-dose of aspirin. There is, however, no direct evidence showing the increased risk of gastric bleeding in the animal model. In the present study, we examined the effect of an anti-platelet drug clopidogrel, a P2Y12 receptor antagonist, on gastric bleeding induced by intraluminal perfusion with aspirin in the rat stomach. Methods: Male SD rats were used after 18 h fasting. Under urethane anesthesia, two catheters were inserted into the stomach, one from an incision in the esophagus and another through the pylorus via an incision in the duodenum. Then, the stomach was superfued with saline at a rate of 0.4 ml/min using an infusion pump, and the perfusate was collected every 5 min. After an equilibration period with saline perfusion for 60 min, the stomach was perfused with acidified aspirin (5-25 mM in 50 mM HCl) for another 60 min. Gastric bleeding was evaluated as the hemoglobin concentration in the perfusate. Clopidogrel (10-100 mg/kg, PO) was given 24 h before the perfusion. Several agents, including omeprazole (30 mg/kg), cimetidine (100 mg/kg), teprenone (300 mg/kg), rebamipide (1∼10 mg/kg) or indomethacin (5 mg/kg) was given ID or SC, respectively, 90 min before the onset of aspirin perfusion, while PGE2 (1 mg/kg, IV) was given 5 min before the aspirin perfusion. Results: Perfusion of the stomach with aspirin dose-dependently provoked gastric bleeding with a significant increase of hemoglobin content in the perfusate. Pretreatment with clopidogrel, despite provoking by itself neither bleeding nor damage even at 100 mg/kg, dose-dependently increased the severity of gastric damage and bleeding caused by aspirin (50 mM). The gastric bleeding caused by clopidogrel plus aspirin was significantly reduced by prior administration of PGE2 and further potentiated by indomethacin. On the other hand, both rebamipide and teprenone significantly reduced the severity of gastric damage as well as bleeding in response to clopidogrel plus aspirin, while neither omeprazole nor cimetidine had any effect. Conclusion: These results suggest that clopidogrel, the anti-platelet drug, increases the gastric bleeding induced by a low dose of aspirin. Both rebamipide and teprenone are useful for preventing the gastric bleeding caused by aspirin plus clopidogrel. This model may be useful for screening of the protective drugs against gastric bleeding.
