N2  - Background/Aim: Antithrombotic therapy takes a central role of the treatment for apoplexy, cardiac disorder and peripheral arterial disease. However, recent clinical studies suggested a risk of gastric adverse reaction on the concomitant use of antithrombotic drugs with nonsteroidal antiinflammatory drugs (NSAIDs). In the present study, we used indomethacin and flurbiprofen as conventional NSAIDs and investigated the adverse effects of antiplatelet drug clopidogrel, a P2Y12 receptor antagonist, on gastric antral ulcers induced by NSAIDs in rats, and investigated the effects of antisecretory drugs and mucosal protective drugs on the occurrence of antral ulcers induced by indomethacin plus clopidogrel. Methods: Male SD rats were used. The animals fasted for 24 h were re-fed for 1 hr, and subsequently administered indomethacin (30 mg/kg) or flurbiprofen (30 mg/kg) SC 1 h after refeeding. Six hours later, the animals were killed and the stomachs were examined for non-hemorrhagic and hemorrhagic lesions, separately. Clopidogrel (3∼30 mg/kg) was given PO 48, 24 h and 30 min before indomethacin treatment. Atropine (3 mg/kg), omeprazole (30 mg/kg), famatidine (1-10 mg/kg), rebamipide (1∼10 mg/kg) or teprenone (30-300 mg/kg) was given PO 1 h before indomethacin. Results: Indomethacin alone produced multiple lesions in the antral mucosa of refed rats. Clopidogrel, despite provoking no damage by itself, dosedependently worsened the severity of these antral lesions in response to indomethacin, with an increase of myeloperoxidase (MPO) activity. Similar results were obtained when clopidogrel was co-administered with flurbiprofen, in place of indomethacin. The antral lesions induced by indomethacin or flurbiprofen alone were mostly non-hemorrhagic, while they became hemorrhagic by the co-administration with clopidogrel. The severity of antral lesions induced by indomethacin plus clopidogrel was dose-dependently and significantly reduced by atropine, omeprazole and famatidine. Likewise, the aggravating effect of clopidogrel was dose-dependently attenuated by the pretreatment with the mucosal protective drugs such as rebamipide or teprenone, with a concomitant suppression of MPO activity. Conclusion: These results suggest that clopidogrel, the anti-platelet drug, aggravates the severity of NSAID-induced antral ulcers in refed rats, mainly converting from non-hemorrhagic lesions into hemorrhagic ones. The antisecretory drugs (omeprazole, famatidine and atropine) as well as the mucosal protective drug (rebamipide and teprenone) are useful for preventing such an adverse reaction in the antral mucosa caused by the co-administration of NSAID and antiplatelet drug.
