N2  - Background: Antiplatelet drug resistance in patients undergoing cerebrovascular stent placement is not well characterized. Since the institution of platelet aggregometry testing in our hospital, we observed that patients who developed peri-procedural thrombotic complications were found to be highly resistant to oral anti-platelet therapy in the standard dosages. Here we characterize the effects of aspirin and clopidogrel on platelet aggregation and analyze the correlation with peri-procedural thrombotic complications. Methods: We used the VerifyNow aspirin assay to calculate aspirin reaction units (ARU) and the VerifyNow P2Y12 assay (Accumetrics, San Diego, CA) to calculate percentage inhibition of platelet function due to clopidogrel immediately prior to the procedures. All complication data were prospectively recorded as per New York state guidelines. We did an all-inclusive retrospective chart review to abstract demographic and additional clinical data for patients who underwent cerebrovas-cular stent placement from August 2006 through December 2008. Possible clinical indicators of peri-procedural complication were analyzed using the Pearson's 2 test. Aspirin resistance was defined as ARU > 550. Clopidogrel resistance was defined as percentage platelet inhibition < 40% as per the cardiology literature. P2Y12 inhibition < 20% was defined as high resistance to clopidogrel. Results: During the study time period, 48 patients underwent 51 stent placement procedures. 27 stents were placed for extracranial stenosis; and 26 intracranial stents were placed for intracranial stenosis or stent assisted coiling. For most patients undergoing elective procedures, aspirin 81mg and clopidogrel 75mg both daily were started 5 days prior to the procedure. Among 49 patients for whom the ARU were available, the mean was 457. A total of 5 (10%) patients were aspirin resistant. Among 49 patients for whom the P2Y12 inhibition was available, the mean platelet inhibition was 32%. A total of 33 (67%) patients were clopidogrel resistant. 16 (33%) patients were highly resistant to clopidogrel. 3 patients developed thrombotic complications after stent placement. Being highly resistant to clopidogrel correlated with development of a thrombotic complication (P<0.03). No other factor was statistically significant. Conclusions: Consistent with the literature, we found over half of the patients receiving clopidogrel were non-therapeutic. Asprin resistance was less common. High resistance to clopidogrel correlates with development of thrombotic complications from cerebrovascular stenting. Further study on the use and degree of platelet inhibition for neurovascular patients is required.
