N2  - Background: Ticagrelor is a novel, reversibly binding oral direct-acting P2Y12-receptor antagonist. In the PLATO trial, which included 18,624 randomized patients with acute coronary syndromes (ACS), ticagrelor compared with clopidogrel significantly reduced the risk of the primary composite endpoint of cardiovascular (CV) death, myocardial infarction or stroke (RR 0.84; 95% CI 0.77-0.92, p<0.001). This report concerns the outcome in patients treated with coronary artery by-pass (CABG) surgery during the trial. Methods: In total, 1,899 patients underwent CABG post randomization. The protocol recommended ticagrelor/placebo to be withheld for 24-72 hrs and clopidogrel/placebo for 5 days. 1,261 patients underwent CABG and were on study drug treatment <7 days prior to surgery. The statistical analysis was based on events occurring from the CABG procedure until the end of the study. Results: In the 1261 patient cohort, the relative reduction of primary composite endpoint [10.5% (66/631) with ticagrelor vs. 12.6% (79/629) with clopidogrel; RR 0.84, 95% CI 0.60-1.16, p=0.29] showed no heterogeneity in comparison to the total trial result at one year. Total mortality was reduced from 9.2% (58/629) to 4.6% (29/631) (RR 0.49 CI 0.32-0.77; P<0.01), cardiovascular (CV) death from 7.5% (47/629) to 4.0% (25/631) (P<0.01) and non-CV death from 1.7% to 0.6% (P=0.07). There was no significant difference in CABG-related major bleedings between the randomized treatments. Conclusions: In patients undergoing CABG within 7 days after the last study drug intake, ticagrelor compared to clopidogrel was associated with a substantial reduction in total and CV mortality without any change in the risk of CABG-related bleeding.
