N2  - Background: High on-treatment platelet reactivity (HPR) is associated with thrombo-ischemic events following percutaneous coronary intervention (PCI). However, the platelet function test that best predicts thrombo-ischemic events is unknown. Methods and Results: The POPular-study enrolled 1069 consecutive patients on clopidogrel-therapy undergoing elective PCI. On-treatment platelet reactivity was measured in parallel by light transmittance aggregometry (LTA), the VerifyNow® P2Y12 assay, the Plateletworks® assay, the IMPACT-R (with and without pre-stimulation with adenosine diphosphate [ADP]) and the PFA-100® System (with the Dade® PFA Collagen/ADP Test Cartridge and the novel INNOVANCE® PFA P2Y∗). Cut-off values for HPR were established by receiver-operator characteristic curve analysis. At one-year follow-up, the primary endpoint-a composite of all-cause death, non-fatal acute myocardial infarction, stent thrombosis and non-fatal ischemic stroke- occurred more frequently in patients with HPR as assessed by LTA (11.7% vs 6.0%, p=0.0009 with 5 μmol/L ADP and 12.0% vs 6.2%, p=0.001 using 20 μmol/L ADP), the VerifyNow® P2Y12 assay (13.3% vs 5.7%, p< 0.0001), the Plateletworks® assay (12.6% vs 6.1%, p=0.005) and INNOVANCE® PFA P2Y∗ (10.8% vs 6.3%, p=0.15). In contrast, the IMPACT-R and the Dade® PFA Collagen/ADP Test Cartridge were unable to discriminate between patients with and without ischemic events at one-year follow-up. Conclusions: The LTA, the VerifyNow® P2Y12 assay and the Plateletworks® are able to identify patients undergoing PCI who are at higher risk for thrombo-ischemic events. The novel INNOVANCE® PFA P2Y∗ seems promising. The IMPACT-R and the Dade® PFA Collagen/ADP Test Cartridge were unable to identify higher-risk patients.
