N2  - Background: Ticagrelor, the first reversible oral P2Y12 receptor antagonist provides faster, greater, and more consistent P2Y12 inhibition and might therefore be an alternative to clopidogrel. The PLATO trial tested whether ticagrelor is superior to clopidogrel for the prevention of vascular events in patients with either non-ST-elevation ACS or ST-elevation ACS with planned primary percutaneous coronary intervention (PCI). Methods: PLATO, a randomized, double-blind, event-driven trial, enrolled 18 624 patients, of whom 7544 had been randomized after presenting with ST-elevation (or new left bundle branch block) ACS and being triaged to primary PCI. Patients were allocated to either ticagrelor 180 mg loading dose followed by 90 mg twice daily, or clopidogrel 300 mg loading dose (with provision for additional 300 mg at PCI) followed by 75 mg daily for 6 to 12 months, on top of aspirin. The primary efficacy variable was time to first occurrence of the MI, stroke, or death from vascular causes. Secondary efficacy variables included (a) all cause mortality, MI, or stroke; (b) the composite of death from vascular causes, MI, stroke, recurrent cardiac ischemia, transient ischemic attack (TIA), or other arterial thrombotic event; (c) individual components of the primary efficacy endpoint; and (d) all-cause mortality. Another predefined endpoint was stent thrombosis. The primary safety variable was the time to first occurrence of major bleeding. Results: PLATO recruited from 862 sites in 43 countries from October 2006 until July 2008. Follow-up was completed in February 2009. Baseline characteristics are summarized in the table. The results of the main trial are planned for first presentation as a Late Breaking Clinical Trial at the European Congress of Cardiology 2009. This presentation will show for the first time the results in the cohort with ST-elevation planned for primary PCI. Additionally, this will be the largest primary PCI population in a randomized trial ever. (Table Presented).
