N2  - Antiplatelet therapy is a mainstay for prevention of thromboembolism (TE) during endovascular procedures. Low response (LR) to antiplatelet therapy, identified using platelet function testing (PFT), may lead to higher rates of TE. This study reviews PFT options and discusses the epidemiology, aetiology and management of antiplatelet agent LR. A systematic PubMed search was conducted for articles addressing aspirin or clopidogrel LR, its management, and comparison of PFT methods with gold standard for LR detection. Articles addressed aspirin (n = 35), clopidogrel LR (n = 31) or both (n = 53), and platelet function testing (n = 92). Cardiovascular patients were the dominant study demographic. Aspirin and clopdiogrel LR was reported in 5-65% and 4-44% of cases respectively. Dual LR was documented in 6% of patients. Neurointervention studies (n = 3) reported aspirin and clopidogrel LR in 2.1-13% and 28-66% respectively. Non-compliance was the major reported cause of LR. Other significant factors included incorrect dose, poor absorption, hepatic cytochrome genetic polymorphisms, female gender, obesity, diabetes mellitus, smoking, hypertension and drug interactions. LR conferred significantly increased rates of peri-procedural stroke, myocardial infarction, stent thrombosis and death compared to normal responders. VerifyNow and PFA-100 represented the most frequently used PFT platform, but were inferior to impedance aggregometry (Chronolog, Multiplate) when compared to gold standard optical aggregometry. LR was addressed by PFT-titrated dose increase, increased therapy duration, delay of procedure, and addition or substitution with other antiplatelet agents such as glycoprotein IIb/IIIa inhibitors. Assessment of pre-procedural platelet inhibition is not yet routine clinical practice. Comprehensive guidelines are needed to establish optimum PFT and establish reference ranges, timing and patient selection guidelines. If LR is documented patient-specific management plans will be required.
