N2  - Recent advances in stroke prevention include the results of the EXPRESS study, which showed that early treatment of TIA reduces stroke risk by 80%. The PROFESS trial made it clear that ASA/dipyridamole is no more effective than clopidogrel. There is new evidence that better control of the INR in patients with atrial fibrillation reduces stroke by half. Despite this, there is also recent evidence that only 10% of patients with high-risk atrial fibrillation (AF) are anticoagulated appropriately (to an INR of 2-3), and only 18% of AF patients with a previous TIA or stroke are appropriately anticoagulated. There is also new evidence that stroke risk in AF rises steeply with age (by 45% per decade), and that antiplatelet agents become less effective in older patients, whereas warfarin remains effective. It is therefore of great importance to find ways to overcome the reluctance of physicians to prescribe, and of patients to take, warfarin. A recent advance is the use of genetic testing to guide warfarin therapy, by assessing genetic variants of the enzyme that metabolizes warfarin, cytochrome P-450 2C9 (CYP2C9), and of a key pharmacologic target of warfarin, vitamin K epoxide reductase (VKORC1). New oral anticoagulants can be expected to replace warfarin, as they will be much easier to use. These include direct thrombin inhibitors such as dabigatran, and Factor Xa inhibitors such as rivaroxaban. Homocysteine promotes formation of red thrombus, markedly increasing the risk of stroke in atrial fibrillation, this explains why vitamin therapy reduces stroke risk. Proton pump inhibitors such as lansoprazole, omeprazole, etc. reduce the efficacy of clopidogrel because they inhibit CYP2C19, preventing the conversion of clopidogrel to its active form. The exception is pantoprazole. Resistant hypertension requires renin/aldosterone ratios.
