C1  - aspirin
N2  - In the last few years, the concept of aspirin resistance has been largely emphasised in the medical literature, although its definition, mechanism, and specific guidelines for its management remain unclear. Aspirin displays good antithrombotic activity. Various laboratory parameters assessing the efficacy of aspirin like bleeding time, platelet reactivity, thromboxane-A2 (TX-A2) production, and measurement of platelet aggregation, have confirmed the lack of its uniform effect on the platelets. Few studies have reported aspirin resistance to the tune of 5 - 45%. Various extrinsic and intrinsic factors influence the resistance. Numerous studies reveal that aspirin resistance can be overcome by combining it with another antithrombotic agent, i.e., clopidogrel. Further, clopidogrel resistance has also been reported. So, much is expected in the field of diagnostic tests in order to know the true picture of aspirin resistance.
