N2  - Aspirin and clopidogrel (Plavix®), two of the most commonly used antiplatelet agents, inhibit platelet function by acetylating cytoplasmic cyclooxygenase and by occupying the membrane ADP receptor P2Y12, respectively. It is well accepted that these antiplatelet agents decrease the incidence of secondary myocardial infarction, stroke, peripheral artery disease and cardiovascular death in vascular patients. However, many patients continue to experience adverse events despite anti-platelet therapy. Two meta-analyses collated a number of case-control retrospective studies and a handful of prospective randomized double-blinded trials that employed laboratory analyses to identify antiplatelet drug response variability. The studies employed platelet-rich plasma optical aggregometry, whole blood impedance aggregometry, Seimens (Dade-Behring) PFA-100 whole blood closure analysis, the whole blood Accumetrics VerifyNow aggregation meter, and urine-based 11-dehydro thromboxane B2 (UDHT). The various platforms demonstrated suboptimal response to aspirin in 27.1% (21.5-32.6) of patients, with a suboptimal response to clopidogrel in 5-35% of cases, a circumstance sometimes called aspirin or clopidogrel (antiplatelet) “resistance.” There was, however, little agreement among the platforms, with only 2% of subjects identified as resistant by all. Suboptimal response is more than a laboratory phenomenon, as several studies have correlated aspirin resistance in vascular disease patients to increased risk of stroke, acute myocardial infarction, and cardiovascular death, and others have documented adverse vascular events in clopidogrel resistance. Curiously, resistance has been consistently linked to adverse outcomes using various platforms despite poor inter-platform agreement. A number of genetic studies have identified potential causes for antiplatelet resistance, including membrane receptor P2Y12 polymorphisms and cytochrome P450 polymorphisms CYP2C19, CYP3A4, CYP3A5, CYP1A2 and CYP2B6. Likewise, clinical studies have linked response variability to inflammation, lipid profile, and platelet turnover. The clinical and laboratory variability is likely multifactorial. At present, there is little evidence to guide therapy in patients with antiplatelet resistance. Responding to the prevalence and risks of aspirin and clopidogrel resistance, several clinical trials have been developed to develop a therapeutic recommendation. Aspirin dose escalation has little effect, and current efforts are directed towards the possibilities of dual antiplatelet drug therapy; an experimental P2Y12 antagonist, prasugrel; or the addition of current antithrombotic drugs such as dipyridamole of cilostazole. Given the inter-assay variability and current lack of firm therapeutic recommendations, the Working Group on Aspirin Resistance, International Society on Thrombosis and Haemostasis has recommended that It is not currently appropriate to test for aspirin “resistance” in patients or to change therapy based on such tests. Answers and recommendations for an appropriate testing and therapeutic regimen are anticipated upon completion of these clinical trials.
