N2  - Results: Clopidogrel is metabolically activated by hepatic cytochrome P450 2C19 (CYP2C19). Patients with nonfunctioning CYP2C19 alleles are less responsive to clopidogrel, and are at higher risk of cardiovascular (CV) events. Proton pump inhibitors (PPIs) inhibit CYP2C19, and interfere with clopidogrel activation and effect. This may be very important for patients with diabetes that are already high-risk and less responsive to clopidogrel. We investigated the impact of PPIs on the effectiveness of clopidogrel to prevent recurrent CV events after coronary stenting in patients with diabetes. This was a population-based retrospective cohort study using a 19-million member integrated claims database. We analyzed 4005 diabetic patients (ICD-9 250) who had undergone percutaneous coronary intervention (PCI) with stenting, and who received clopidogrel alone (N=2238) or with a PPI (N=1767) for the incidence of the composite primary end point of hospitalization for myocardial infarction, unstable angina, stroke/transient ischemic attack, PCI/coronary artery bypass graft, or CV death over 12-months. Event risk was determined by Kaplan-Meier analysis, and hazard ratios from multivariate Cox models. Patients on PPIs were older, more often female, and had greater baseline comorbidity. The primary end point rate was 19.7% in patients not receiving a PPI and 26.7% in patients on combined clopidogrel and PPI therapy (hazard ratio 1.44, 95% confidence interval 1.22-1.69, p<0.0001). Excess risk with PPIs was determined primarily by myocardial infarction/unstable angina [10.2% vs. 16.8%; hazard ratio 1.66 (1.34-2.06), P<0.0001]. Our findings suggest a clinically significant drug interaction between PPIs and clopidogrel resulting in a higher incidence of major adverse cardiac events within one year after coronary stenting in patients with diabetes.[figure1]. (Diagram presented).
