N2  - Background/Aim: Antithrombotic therapy takes a central role of the treatment for apoplexy, cardiac disorder and peripheral arterial disease. However, recent clinical studies suggested a risk of gastric adverse reaction on the concomitant use of antithrombotic drugs with nonsteroidal anti-inflammatory drugs (NSAIDs). In the present study, we used indomethacin and flurbiprofen as conventional NSAIDs and investigated the adverse effects of antiplatelet drug clopidogrel, a P2Y12 receptor antagonist, on gastric antral ulcers induced by NSAIDs in rats, and investigated the effects ofantisecretory drugs and rebamipide,amucosal protective drug, on the occurrence of antral ulcers induced by indomethacin plus antiplatelet drug. Methods: Male SD rats were used. The animals fasted for 24 h were re-fed for 1 hr, and subsequently administered indomethacin (30 mg/kg) or flurbiprofen (30 mg/kg) SC 1 h after refeeding. Six hours later, the animals were killed and the stomachs were examined for non-hemorrhagic and hemorrhagic lesions, separately. Clopidogrel (3∼30 mg/kg) was given PO 48, 24 h and 30 min before indomethacin treatment. Atropine (3 mg/kg), omeprazole (30 mg/kg) or rebamipide (1∼10 mg/kg) was given SC, IP or PO, respectively, 1 h before indomethacin. Myeloperoxidase (MPO) activity was measured 6 h after indomethacin treatment. Results: Indomethacin by itself produced multiple lesions in the antral mucosa of refed rats. Clopidogrel, despite provoking no damage by itself, dose-dependently worsened the severity of these antral lesions in response to indomethacin, with an increase of MPO activity. Similar results were obtained when clopidogrel was co-administered with flurbiprofen, another NSAID, in place of indomethacin. The antral lesions induced by indomethacin alone were mostly non- hemorrhagic, while they became hemorrhagic by the co-administration with clopidogrel. The severity of antral lesions induced by indomethacin plus clopidogrel was significantly reduced by atropine and omeprazole. Likewise, the aggravating effect of clopidogrel was dose-dependently and significantly attenuated by the pretreatment with rebamipide, the mucosal protective drug, with a concomitant suppression of MPO activity. Conclusion: These results suggest that clopidogrel, the anti-platelet drug, aggravates the severity of NSAID-induced antral ulcers in refed rats, mainly converting from nonhemorrhagic lesions into hemorrhagic ones. The antisecretory drugs such as omeprazole and atropine as well as a mucosal protective drug rebamipide are useful for preventing such an adverse reaction in the antral mucosa caused by the co-administration of NSAID and antiplatelet drug.
