N2  - Background: The prodrug Clopidogrel requires activation by cytochrome P-450 (CYP) enzymes for its antiplatelet effect. The genes encoding CYP enzymes are polymorphic, leading to reduced or increased function, depending on the respective genotype. Reduced function alleles have been associated with an increase in cardiovascular events. Methods: We tested the association between the CYP 2C19 ∗17 (C/T) T-allele leading to increased function and further cardiovascular events in a high risk population of 925 patients with acute myocardial infarction. Genotyping was performed with the PCR based TaqMan assay. Results: Carriers of the protective T-allele had a relative decrease of 18% in the composite primary outcome of the risk of death from cardiovascular causes, myocardial infarction, definite stent thrombosis or target vessel revascularisation (MACE, p=0.047), and the composite end-point of MACE or stroke (p=0.049), as compared with noncarriers, respectively. Conclusions: For the first time we found that in this high risk population of patients treated for acute myocardial infarction with clopidogrel, carriers of an increased-function CYP2C19 ∗17 T-allele had significantly lower rates of major adverse cardiovascular events, including stent thrombosis, than did noncarriers.
