N2  - Introduction: Aspirin resistance has a reported incidence as high as 40.1 Excimer laser-assisted non-occlusive anastomosis (ELANA) was developed to avoid temporary occlusion of vessels during EC-IC bypass.2 This procedure avoids the inherent risk of occlusion time and the associated cerebral ischemia. Patients are preoperatively placed on aspirin regimens to promote increased blood flow, decreased blood viscosity, and thrombosis. Methods: After IRB approved informed consent, 16 patients were placed on 325mg of aspirin 2 days prior to surgery. On the morning of surgery, a VerifyNow Aspirin assay (Accumetrics San Diego, CA) was utilized to evaluate the effectiveness of aspirin therapy. Asprin prevents platelet activation by irreversibly binding to the COX-1 enzyme and preventing the conversion of arachidonic acid to thromboxane A2. Activation of GP IIb/IIIa receptors which are ultimately involved in platelet aggregation is inhibited. The VerifyNow Asprin Assay uses arachidonic acid as an agonist to measure platelet activation along this pathway. If the aspirin is effective in preventing conversion of arachidonic acid to thromboxane A2, the pathway is inhibited and platelets cannot aggregate. The assay determines this by measuring the rate and extent of changes in light transmittance caused by platelets aggregating in whole blood samples. Blood samples with inhibited platelets will produce a low level of light transmittance, while samples containing normally functioning platelets will aggregate more rapidly, resulting in a higher level of light transmittance. The results of this assay are quantified in Aspirin Reaction Units (ARU). Aspirin was considered to be therapeutic when less than 550 ARU were detected.3 All patients with ARU >550 were placed on coumadin or Plavix immediately postoperatively. Results: Table 1 shows the ARU for intracranial EC-IC bypass surgeries. The assay revealed 3 out of 16 patients (18.8) had ARU above 550. There were no thrombotic perioperative complications in any patient. Discussion: The occurrence of perioperative thrombosis can produce devastating complications including re-operations, extended ICU care, strokes, and death. The ELANA procedure offers advantages to traditional intracranial bypass techniques. Our preliminary study shows that there may need to be a concern over the presence of aspirin resistance, not only in candidates for the ELANA procedure, but also for any patient about to undergo a vascular bypass operation. Alberts et al found that 56 of patients taking low-dose aspirin (< 162 mg/dl) had normal platelet function compared with 28 of those taking >325 mg/d of aspirin.4 The study also found that 65 of patients taking enteric-coated aspirin had normal platelet function compared TABLE 1. Age Sex Operation ARU 59 F L STA-MCA Bypass 576 60 M EC-IC Bypass 383 57 M PICA/PICA Bypass 410 68 M M2-P2 ELANA Vein Bypass 474 53 F L STA-MCA Bypass 467 54 F EC-IC Bypass 425 58 M R STA-MCA Bypass 455 55 M M2/M2 IC-IC Bypass 504 STA-MCA EC-IC Bypass 70 F L STA-MCA Bypass 655 70 F R STA-MCA Bypass 600 80 M R Occipital to PICA Bypass 499 59 M R EC-P2 Bypass 484 48 M R STA-MCA Bypass 470 51 F PICA-PICA Bypass 453 59 M EC-IC Bypass 459 65 M R STA-MCA Bypass 452 with 25 taking an uncoated preparation. Our sample size prevents unambiguous conclusions as we further explore thrombotic complications during and following ELANA procedures. Conclusion: Aspirin resistance may be useful in informing therapeutic strategies for prevention of thrombotic complications in neurosurgical vascular bypass procedures.
