N2  - Background: Our genetic studies have identified a sequence variant in the PTGER3 gene encoding the EP3 receptor for prostaglandin E2 as a risk factor for PAD, MI and stroke. DG-041 is a potent and selective antagonist of the EP3 receptor, currently under development for the treatment of atherothrombosis. EP3 is a G1 coupled receptor, which upon activation predisposes the platelet towards an activated state. The EP3 pathway is currently not protected by any anti-platelet therapy. Vasodilator - stimulated phosphoprotein (VASP) is a major substrate for cAMP/cGMP protein kinases in platelets. Phosphorylation of VASP is a significant check-point regulator of platelet activation, where an increase in pVASP correlates with inhibition of platelets. VASP is also involved in the down-regulation of platelet adhesion to the vascular wall under both physiological and pathophysiological conditions. Here we examine the effects of EP3 agonist mediated dephosphorylation of pVASP and the protection afforded by DG-041 under various experimental in-vitro conditions. Methods: A sensitive pVASP ELISA method was developed to detect low levels of pVASP in platelet-rich-plasma (PRP). The ELISA method was used to measure pVASP levels in PRP after in-vitro stimulation under different conditions. Platelet aggregation was performed using light transmission aggregometry. Results: DG-041 afforded protection of pVASP in a concentration-dependent manner with an EC50 of 207 nM. The effect of DG-041 on pVASP highly correlated with the effect of DG-041 on the inhbition of collagen/sulprostone mediated platelet aggregation (EC50 of 219 nM). EP3 signaling and the potency of platelet inhibition by DG-041 was not affected in samples from PAD patients taking either ASA or the P2Y12 anti-platelet agent Plavix. Conclusions: DG-041 prevented EP3 stimulated platelet activation as measured by pVASP. The effect of DG-041 on pVASP closely correlated with its inhibitory effect on platelet aggregation. EP3-dependent platelet activation as measured by pVASP was not affected by current anti-platelet therapies in PAD patients nor was there any effect of these agents on protection provided by DG-041. pVASP has utility as a biomarker of DG-041 treatment effects in human clinical trials.
