C1  - aspirin
N2  - Aspirin is an antithrombotic drug that has analgesic, antipyretic, anti-inflammatory, and antithrombotic effects. Salicylates act by irreversibly inhibiting platelet cyclooxygenase (COX-1). As a consequence, platelets lose their ability to synthesize thromboxane A2, which is necessary for effective platelet aggregation. Because platelets play a key role in thrombosis, aspirin is widely used in cardiac disease prophylaxis. Clinical trials have shown that thromboembolic vascular events are reduced by approximately 25% with daily aspirin use. The American Heart Association recommends aspirin use for all patients who have cardiovascular disease. Because of gastric bleeding, low doses of aspirin (i.e., 81 mg) are recommended. There is a subset of all individuals, estimated to be between 5.5% and 60%, who are resistant to the antiplatelet effects of aspirin. The underlying pathophysiology of aspirin has not been identified. Among polymorphisms that have been studied include genes for glycoprotein IIb/IIIa, collagen, and von Willebrand factor receptors. There may also be metabolic and cellular factors that reduce local aspirin drug concentrations resulting in resistance. Compared to patients who are aspirin sensitive, resistant patients have an increased rate of adverse cardiovascular events such as acute myocardial infarction (AMI) and stroke. Resistant patients who are treated prophylactically with low-dose aspirin are inadequately protected against platelet-induced thrombotic events. The use of higher aspirin dosage or the use of more potent antiplatelet drugs such as dopidogrel, are necessary to effectively inhibit platelet function. Patients who are also resistant to clopidogrel may require more aggressive measures of therapeutic or surgical cardioprotection. Salicylate and clopidogrel resistance can be determined by aggregometry or through urinary measurement of 11-dehydrothromboxane B2 (thromboxane A2 metabolite). ©2007 Clinical Ligand Assay Society. All rights reserved.
